QUESTIONS.push(

  // ================= Mission Mode Projects & Digital Platforms (50) =================
  { id: "mmp-01", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "A Mission Mode Project (MMP) under the National e-Governance Plan is best defined as:", options: ["A short-term departmental IT pilot that carries no defined ownership, timelines or measurable service targets of its own","An individual e-governance project with defined objectives, scope, timelines and measurable service-delivery outcomes, owned by a specific ministry, department or state","A privately funded software product marketed to government offices, with no formal government ownership, no milestone-based reporting, and no accountability to any specific ministry, department or state","An annual departmental budget line item that is reviewed once a year, without any milestone or outcome targets ever attached to it"], answer: 1, exp: "MMP status specifically implies clear milestones, ownership and measurable outcomes, not an unscoped IT purchase or budget line." },
  { id: "mmp-02", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "The National e-Governance Plan (NeGP), under which all MMPs are organized, was approved with the core objective of:", options: ["Prioritizing internet-based access for central government staff first, before the general public is added in a later, unscheduled phase of rollout","Consolidating several government departments under a single private outsourcing contractor for their entire day-to-day administration and staffing","Making government services accessible to the common man through common service delivery outlets, and ensuring efficiency, transparency and reliability of such services at affordable cost","Requiring every government office to stop accepting paper applications within a fixed deadline, regardless of citizens' digital access or readiness, with no parallel offline channel retained"], answer: 2, exp: "NeGP's stated objective centers on citizen-accessible, efficient, transparent and affordable service delivery through ICT." },
  { id: "mmp-03", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Mission Mode Projects are classified into how many categories, based on which level of government owns/implements them?", options: ["Three — Central, State, and Integrated","Two categories, split strictly by which level funds it","Five categories, defined loosely by sector","One category, with no further sub-classification"], answer: 0, exp: "MMPs fall into three categories — Central, State and Integrated — based on which level(s) of government own and implement them." },
  { id: "mmp-04", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Which of the following is a Central Mission Mode Project?", options: ["Land Records, since it is run individually by each state's own revenue department","Employment Exchange, since it is run by state-level labour boards, not a union body","Common Services Centres, since they are jointly run across central and state levels","Income Tax (e-filing), administered by a union department"], answer: 3, exp: "Income tax e-filing is administered by the Central Board of Direct Taxes, a union government body, making it a Central MMP." },
  { id: "mmp-05", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "The Passport Seva Project, a Central MMP, is notable for being implemented through:", options: ["A state-run helpdesk model, with day-to-day issuance handled directly by district administrations rather than any dedicated central passport office","A public-private partnership model, with a private service provider running Passport Seva Kendras under the Ministry of External Affairs' oversight","Full outsourcing of the passport-issuing authority itself to a private logistics contractor","An in-house model staffed solely by ministry personnel, with no outside service partner involved"], answer: 1, exp: "Passport Seva combined private-sector service delivery at Passport Seva Kendras with continued government control over passport issuance authority." },
  { id: "mmp-06", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "MCA21, a Central MMP under the Ministry of Corporate Affairs, primarily enables:", options: ["Case-listing and hearing-schedule management for district and high courts across every state and union territory","Digitization of state land records and mutation entries maintained at the tehsil level","Assessment and collection of municipal property tax records by urban local bodies","Online company/LLP registration, statutory filing and related corporate compliance services"], answer: 3, exp: "MCA21 digitizes company/LLP registration and statutory compliance filings for the Ministry of Corporate Affairs." },
  { id: "mmp-07", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Central Excise, one of the original Central Mission Mode Projects, digitized:", options: ["Disbursement of state-level agricultural input subsidies directly to registered farmers","Billing and metering records for municipal water-supply connections in urban areas","Excise duty administration and compliance processes for the union government","Filing and tracking of criminal case records maintained across district courts"], answer: 2, exp: "Central Excise as an MMP focused on digitizing union government excise duty administration and compliance." },
  { id: "mmp-08", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Bhoomi, Karnataka's computerized land records system, is a well-known example of which MMP category?", options: ["State MMP, since land records are owned and implemented at the state level","Central MMP, since land titling is treated as a union subject applied nationwide","Integrated MMP, since it is jointly maintained by the centre and the states","Not an MMP at all, since it predates the NeGP framework by several decades"], answer: 0, exp: "Land Records is a State MMP, owned and implemented individually by each state government — Bhoomi is Karnataka's implementation." },
  { id: "mmp-09", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "The e-District Mission Mode Project primarily aims to:", options: ["Track only central government pension files and their disbursement schedules, with no involvement in any certificate, license or grievance-redress service","Provide cloud hosting infrastructure exclusively to state-level data centres","Serve as a full replacement for the Passport Seva Project's Kendra network","Deliver a range of citizen-centric services (certificates, licenses, grievance redress) electronically at the district administration level"], answer: 3, exp: "e-District is a State MMP delivering routine citizen-facing certificate, licensing and grievance services at the district level." },
  { id: "mmp-10", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Which MMP category does 'Commercial Taxes' belong to?", options: ["Integrated, since commercial tax administration is shared jointly across levels","State, since it is owned and administered by individual state governments","Central, since it is run by a single dedicated union revenue body","Not classified under any of the three recognized MMP categories"], answer: 1, exp: "Commercial Taxes is a State MMP, administered individually by each state's tax department." },
  { id: "mmp-11", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Employment Exchange, as a Mission Mode Project, is classified as:", options: ["An Integrated MMP, run jointly across central and state boards","A Central MMP, run directly by a union ministry alone","A State MMP","Not formally recognized as any kind of MMP"], answer: 2, exp: "Employment Exchange is implemented and owned by individual state governments, making it a State MMP." },
  { id: "mmp-12", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "e-Courts, a Mission Mode Project computerizing India's judiciary, is classified as:", options: ["A Central MMP only, run by a union department with no state courts involved","A State MMP only, since each high court manages its own district courts separately","Not a formally recognized MMP, though it functions much like one in practice","An Integrated MMP, since it spans both central and state judicial administration"], answer: 3, exp: "e-Courts requires coordinated central-state implementation across the judiciary, making it an Integrated MMP." },
  { id: "mmp-13", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Common Services Centres (CSC), an Integrated MMP, primarily function as:", options: ["Physical access points, often in rural areas, through which citizens can avail multiple government and other digital services","Regional data centres built specifically to host central ministries' internal records, with no citizen-facing access point of any kind","A nationwide banking network that operates with no government participation at all","Dedicated courtroom facilities built specifically to support e-Courts hearings"], answer: 0, exp: "CSCs are last-mile physical access points delivering a bundle of government and other digital services to citizens, especially in rural areas." },
  { id: "mmp-14", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "UID/Aadhaar is categorized as an Integrated MMP primarily because:", options: ["It is used mainly by a single state government for its own welfare schemes and payouts, with no bearing on any central government service or authentication need","Its identity infrastructure is used across both central and state government services, requiring coordinated implementation at both levels","It falls outside all three of the recognized MMP categories entirely","It is designed, funded and operated solely by a private technology vendor"], answer: 1, exp: "Aadhaar's identity layer underpins services across both central and state governments, which is why it is classified as Integrated." },
  { id: "mmp-15", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "GST, delivered through the GST Network (GSTN), is an Integrated MMP because:", options: ["It is administered entirely within a single state's own tax department","It was designed specifically to fully replace every other Mission Mode Project across the entire NeGP portfolio at once","Tax administration under GST spans both central and state tax authorities, requiring integrated implementation","It has no formal link whatsoever to the Mission Mode Project framework"], answer: 2, exp: "GST merged central and state indirect taxes into one system, needing coordinated central-state administration through GSTN." },
  { id: "mmp-16", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "The e-Trade Mission Mode Project primarily focuses on:", options: ["Coordinating waste-collection schedules across urban municipal corporations, a function handled separately by local bodies rather than a central-state MMP","Managing job-vacancy listings for state-run employment exchange offices","Digitizing case filings and hearing dates for district-level courts","Facilitating and digitizing documentation/processes involved in international trade, cutting across multiple central and state agencies"], answer: 3, exp: "e-Trade streamlines the multi-agency documentation involved in cross-border trade, which is why it is an Integrated MMP." },
  { id: "mmp-17", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "The Digitization India Platform (DIP) is best described as:", options: ["A national framework for converting government's physical/scanned records, documents and archives into structured, reusable digital data","A citizen-facing document wallet that functions much like DigiLocker's own issued-documents feature, rather than a back-office digitization tool","A cloud hosting arrangement set up specifically for the Passport Seva Project's own servers","An outreach platform used mainly to publish official government press releases"], answer: 0, exp: "DIP is specifically about digitizing government's own back-office records so they become structured, reusable data — distinct from citizen-facing DigiLocker." },
  { id: "mmp-18", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "One of DigiLocker's core features, 'issued documents', refers to:", options: ["Documents a citizen scans and uploads themselves, without any verification, digital signature, or endorsement from the actual issuing authority concerned in each case","Official documents pushed directly into a citizen's locker by the issuing department/organization (e.g. a digitally signed certificate), carrying the same validity as the original","Documents that, once stored, can never be shared with any third party under any circumstances","Physical paper documents that are mailed to the citizen after being requested online"], answer: 1, exp: "Issued documents in DigiLocker are digitally signed originals pushed by the issuing authority, distinct from self-uploaded scans." },
  { id: "mmp-19", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "The 'eSign' facility within the DigiLocker ecosystem allows a citizen to:", options: ["Physically print and sign a paper copy before scanning it back into the locker app","Only view documents already stored by the issuing department, with no digital signing option offered at all","Digitally sign an electronic document online using Aadhaar-based authentication, giving it legal validity","Permanently remove government-issued documents from the official digital record"], answer: 2, exp: "eSign lets a citizen apply a legally valid digital signature to an electronic document via Aadhaar-based authentication." },
  { id: "mmp-20", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Besides officially issued documents, DigiLocker also lets a citizen:", options: ["Issue fresh Aadhaar numbers on UIDAI's behalf to other registered citizens directly","Approve pending passport applications submitted by other citizens on the portal","View documents stored in another citizen's locker without ever needing their explicit consent first","Upload their own scanned copies of documents for personal safekeeping and easy retrieval"], answer: 3, exp: "DigiLocker's self-upload feature lets citizens store their own document scans alongside officially issued ones." },
  { id: "mmp-21", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "GI Cloud, branded 'MeghRaj', refers to:", options: ["India's Government Community Cloud strategy, built on a panel of empanelled cloud service providers for government use","A single central government data centre that never relies on any outside cloud provider","A commercial cloud subscription that is marketed directly to individual citizens for personal photo and file storage needs","A private company's proprietary internal cloud used only for its own internal operations"], answer: 0, exp: "MeghRaj is a Government Community Cloud strategy relying on empanelled providers, not a single data centre or public commercial offering." },
  { id: "mmp-22", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Among GI Cloud MeghRaj's stated goals is:", options: ["Deliberately slowing ICT rollout so that departments can build internal capacity before adopting any cloud infrastructure at all","Faster deployment of ICT-enabled services to citizens by letting departments provision infrastructure on demand rather than building their own data centres","Barring any government department from ever provisioning infrastructure on cloud","Restricting empanelled cloud use to just one single designated ministry at a time"], answer: 1, exp: "MeghRaj's core goal is enabling faster, on-demand ICT service delivery instead of each department building isolated infrastructure." },
  { id: "mmp-23", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "A key cost-related goal of GI Cloud MeghRaj is:", options: ["Requiring each individual department to build and maintain its own dedicated data centre","Directing a fixed share of ICT spending toward a single chosen private data-centre vendor, regardless of actual departmental need or usage patterns","Optimizing government ICT spending by sharing infrastructure across departments instead of each building redundant, underutilized data centres","Removing departments' need to plan or track their own annual ICT budgets"], answer: 2, exp: "MeghRaj aims at cost optimization through shared infrastructure, avoiding redundant, underutilized departmental data centres." },
  { id: "mmp-24", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "GI Cloud MeghRaj helps government applications handle unpredictable demand spikes (e.g. exam result portals) primarily through:", options: ["Provisioning fixed, non-adjustable server capacity sized only for average daily traffic","Asking citizens to stagger their access to services strictly during off-peak night hours","Limiting each government application to run on a single dedicated physical server","Scalable cloud infrastructure that can be scaled up or down based on actual demand"], answer: 3, exp: "Cloud elasticity/scalability under MeghRaj lets applications handle demand spikes without over-provisioning fixed infrastructure." },
  { id: "mmp-25", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Cloud service providers join the GI Cloud MeghRaj panel by:", options: ["Undergoing an empanelment process, including security audit/compliance checks, before being authorized to host government applications","Simply registering a company name with a nodal agency, with no further technical review","Paying a recurring listing fee each year to appear on a vendor directory","Being nominated informally by an already-empanelled provider, without any independent security audit or compliance review of its own"], answer: 0, exp: "Empanelment under MeghRaj requires providers to pass security audit and compliance checks before hosting government workloads." },
  { id: "mmp-26", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "e-Kranti, described as 'NeGP 2.0', primarily represents:", options: ["A complete abandonment of the original Mission Mode Project approach in favour of entirely standalone, state-run schemes","An expansion and modernization of NeGP's MMP-based approach, incorporating cloud, mobile and a broader set of MMPs","A rebranding exercise limited only to renaming existing MMPs, with no change in scope","A program focused solely on defense-related IT systems and classified infrastructure"], answer: 1, exp: "e-Kranti builds on and expands NeGP's original MMP approach rather than discarding it." },
  { id: "mmp-27", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "e-Kranti's guiding design principle, 'Transformation and not Translation', means:", options: ["Converting an existing paper form into an identical online PDF with the exact same fields","Translating government forms into regional languages only, with no change made to the underlying process or approval steps themselves","Redesigning government processes for genuinely improved service delivery, rather than just digitizing the old paper-based process unchanged","Replacing an already-online process with an entirely manual, in-person alternative"], answer: 2, exp: "The principle calls for redesigning processes for real improvement, not merely digitizing an unchanged paper workflow." },
  { id: "mmp-28", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Compared to NeGP's original set of Mission Mode Projects, e-Kranti's scope:", options: ["Was scaled back sharply to a single flagship MMP covering every sector","Stayed numerically and technologically identical to NeGP's original list, with no new MMPs added under e-Kranti","Dropped every single MMP that had existed before e-Kranti was launched","Was substantially expanded, adding new MMPs and emphasizing newer technologies like cloud and mobile-based delivery"], answer: 3, exp: "e-Kranti significantly broadened the MMP portfolio and pushed adoption of cloud and mobile delivery channels." },
  { id: "mmp-29", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "A newly launched service letting applicants track their passport application status online was developed and is owned by the Ministry of External Affairs. It should be classified as a:", options: ["Central MMP, since it is owned and implemented by a union ministry","State MMP, since status tracking is delegated to state regional offices instead","Integrated MMP, since it is jointly managed by central and state agencies together","Not an MMP at all, since tracking features are treated as minor add-ons"], answer: 0, exp: "Ownership by a union ministry (External Affairs) makes this a Central MMP, regardless of the specific feature involved." },
  { id: "mmp-30", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "The correct basis for classifying an MMP as Central, State, or Integrated is:", options: ["Which specific office building the citizen physically visits to submit an application","Which level(s) of government own, fund and implement the project","Which particular programming framework or database the application happens to be built on","Whether the project also offers a downloadable mobile app alongside its website"], answer: 1, exp: "Classification depends on which level(s) of government own and implement the project, not incidental technical or interaction details." },
  { id: "mmp-31", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "The Passport Seva Project's public-private partnership model involves:", options: ["Complete transfer of the passport-issuing authority itself to the private-sector partner, removing the Ministry of External Affairs from any oversight role in the process entirely","An arrangement that involves no meaningful role for any private-sector partner at all","A private IT service provider operating Passport Seva Kendras and back-end systems under the Ministry of External Affairs' oversight, while passport issuance authority remains with the government","A state government running Kendras entirely independently, with no central coordination"], answer: 2, exp: "The PPP model keeps sovereign issuance authority with government while a private partner runs the operational front-end/back-end." },
  { id: "mmp-32", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "CCTNS (Crime and Criminal Tracking Network & Systems), a Mission Mode Project connecting police stations nationwide, primarily supports:", options: ["Assessment and collection of municipal property tax records across every urban local body in the state","Filing and processing of corporate registration and LLP compliance documents","Issuance and renewal of passports through the network of Passport Seva Kendras","Digitized crime records, investigation tracking and information-sharing across police stations and states"], answer: 3, exp: "CCTNS links police stations nationwide for shared crime-record and investigation data." },
  { id: "mmp-33", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Two MMPs deliver similar citizen services (issuing certificates), but one is Central and the other State. What most likely explains the difference?", options: ["One is owned/implemented by a union ministry, the other by an individual state government","One relies on a relational database, while the other uses a simple flat file system","One charges a processing fee, while the other is offered entirely free of cost","They are functionally identical projects in every respect, differing only in the name chosen at launch"], answer: 0, exp: "MMP classification tracks ownership/implementation level, not technical implementation details or cost." },
  { id: "mmp-34", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Integrated MMPs like GST and UID/Aadhaar require coordinated implementation across levels of government primarily because:", options: ["Coordination between the two levels of government is explicitly discouraged under NeGP's own stated project guidelines","Their underlying data, processes or authentication needs are shared and used by both central and state agencies","Only citizens ever interact with these systems, with no government body running them","These systems have no defined connection to any recognized government function"], answer: 1, exp: "Shared data/process needs across central and state agencies is exactly why Integrated MMPs require coordinated implementation." },
  { id: "mmp-35", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Documents issued through DigiLocker carry the same legal validity as their physical counterparts primarily because of:", options: ["An informal administrative circular that carries no binding legal force of its own","A private certifying company's own internal terms of service accepted by users","The IT Act 2000's legal recognition of electronic records and digital signatures","The Copyright Act 1957's provisions covering literary and artistic works"], answer: 2, exp: "DigiLocker documents rely on the IT Act's recognition of electronic records/digital signatures for their legal validity." },
  { id: "mmp-36", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "A key difference between GI Cloud MeghRaj and a general-purpose commercial public cloud is:", options: ["MeghRaj is not subject to any defined security or compliance requirements at all","MeghRaj providers may host applications only for private-sector clients, and are never permitted to host any government department's applications","There is effectively no meaningful difference between the two kinds of arrangement","MeghRaj providers are empanelled specifically to meet government security/compliance requirements for hosting public-sector applications"], answer: 3, exp: "MeghRaj's defining feature is empanelment against government-specific security/compliance requirements." },
  { id: "mmp-37", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Mission Mode Projects under NeGP are generally expected to be tracked against:", options: ["Clear milestones and measurable service-delivery outcomes, monitored by designated apex/oversight bodies","No formally defined milestones at all, with progress judged informally by each department","Undocumented status updates shared only verbally, with no written record ever kept","A schedule set unilaterally by the implementing vendor, with no government sign-off required at any stage"], answer: 0, exp: "NeGP's MMP approach explicitly requires milestone-based, monitored implementation, not informal or vendor-only tracking." },
  { id: "mmp-38", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "A state government builds its own online portal (using state funds and staff) to digitize property tax records for its municipalities. This is best classified as a:", options: ["Central MMP, run by a union department","State MMP","Integrated MMP, run jointly","Not an MMP, being municipal-only"], answer: 1, exp: "State funding, ownership and implementation makes this a State MMP." },
  { id: "mmp-39", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "The Digitization India Platform's main value to Mission Mode Projects is that it:", options: ["Issues fresh Aadhaar numbers directly to citizens applying through various MMPs","Provides cloud hosting infrastructure exclusively reserved for MMP application servers, unrelated to any record digitization activity","Lets departments reuse already-digitized government records instead of each MMP separately scanning and re-keying the same historical data","Removes the need for any MMP to ever collect new data at all going forward"], answer: 2, exp: "DIP's value is enabling reuse of already-digitized records across MMPs, avoiding duplicated scanning/re-keying effort." },
  { id: "mmp-40", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Which of the following is NOT one of the recognized Mission Mode Project categories?", options: ["Central category","State category","Integrated category","Private"], answer: 3, exp: "The three recognized MMP categories are Central, State and Integrated — there is no separate 'Private' MMP category." },
  { id: "mmp-41", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Why is GST implemented as an Integrated MMP rather than purely a Central or State one?", options: ["Because GST replaced both central taxes (like excise) and state taxes (like VAT/commercial tax) with a single system requiring joint central-state administration","Because GST is entirely disconnected from any recognized form of taxation activity","Because GSTN interacts only directly with citizens, never with any government body","Because GST is managed solely by a private technology vendor, with no government oversight at either the central or the individual state level"], answer: 0, exp: "GST subsumed both central and state indirect taxes, which is why its administration is jointly integrated across levels." },
  { id: "mmp-42", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Regarding the total number of Mission Mode Projects over time, which statement is most accurate?", options: ["The number of MMPs has stayed exactly fixed since NeGP's inception, with no additions made at any point since its founding","NeGP began with a defined set of MMPs across the three categories, and this set was substantially expanded when e-Kranti (NeGP 2.0) was introduced","MMPs were reduced to zero once e-Kranti was rolled out nationwide across every state","Only Central MMPs have ever actually existed, with no State or Integrated category defined"], answer: 1, exp: "e-Kranti substantially expanded NeGP's original MMP portfolio rather than leaving it unchanged or eliminating it." },
  { id: "mmp-43", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "If a citizen uploads a scanned copy of a privately issued rent agreement to their DigiLocker, this falls under which DigiLocker feature?", options: ["Issued documents feature, since it is the same as self-upload","eSign facility, meant for signing rather than storage","Self-uploaded documents","GI Cloud MeghRaj, a separate hosting platform"], answer: 2, exp: "A privately obtained document scanned and uploaded by the citizen themself falls under DigiLocker's self-uploaded documents feature, not issued documents." },
  { id: "mmp-44", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Which best distinguishes GI Cloud 'MeghRaj' from the Digitization India Platform (DIP)?", options: ["They are simply two different names used for the exact same underlying platform","MeghRaj digitizes old paper records while DIP instead provides cloud hosting infrastructure, which is the exact reverse of their actual, officially defined roles under the NeGP and e-Kranti frameworks","Neither platform has any stated connection to the Mission Mode Project framework at all","MeghRaj provides shared cloud computing/hosting infrastructure for government applications, while DIP focuses on converting government's existing physical records into reusable digital data — they solve different problems"], answer: 3, exp: "MeghRaj is cloud infrastructure; DIP is about digitizing existing physical records — distinct, complementary roles." },
  { id: "mmp-45", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "'Banking', as one of the original Central Mission Mode Projects, focused on:", options: ["Digitizing and interconnecting banking services/infrastructure at a national level, an area the union government coordinates given banking's national regulatory framework","Computerizing records for village-level cooperative credit societies alone, across every district panchayat in the state, with no coverage of nationalized or private banks","Automating municipal water-billing and household connection records on a statewide basis","Digitizing land-record registers that are maintained individually by state revenue departments"], answer: 0, exp: "Banking as a Central MMP reflects the union-level regulatory and infrastructure coordination role in national banking services." },
  { id: "mmp-46", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "The 'Pension' Mission Mode Project is categorized as Central primarily because:", options: ["Pension records are handled only at the municipal corporation level nationwide","Central government pension administration and disbursement is coordinated at the union level","It functions as a privately underwritten insurance scheme, with no government role in it at all","It is implemented independently and unevenly by each state, with no central coordination"], answer: 1, exp: "Central government pension administration is coordinated at the union level, placing this MMP in the Central category." },
  { id: "mmp-47", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "e-Procurement and e-Biz (aimed at easing business setup/compliance) are best classified as:", options: ["Central MMPs only, since business registration is treated purely as a union subject","State MMPs only, since each individual state issues its own separate business registration and licensing paperwork","Integrated MMPs, since they typically involve processes and approvals spanning multiple government levels/agencies","Schemes run entirely outside the MMP framework, managed instead by industry bodies"], answer: 2, exp: "Business setup/compliance processes usually cut across central and state approvals, making these Integrated MMPs." },
  { id: "mmp-48", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "The main reason Mission Mode Projects are encouraged to build on shared platforms like DIP, DigiLocker and GI Cloud MeghRaj, rather than each building separate infrastructure, is to:", options: ["To deliberately slow project timelines so that departments can first build up internal technical capacity on their own","To prevent smaller departments from ever bringing a project fully into production","To restrict shared-platform access to just a single ministry at any given time","Avoid duplicated effort/cost and ensure faster, more consistent, interoperable service delivery across projects"], answer: 3, exp: "Shared platforms reduce duplicated effort and improve consistency/interoperability across many MMPs." },
  { id: "mmp-49", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "e-Kranti's expanded MMP framework is best understood as supporting which broader national program?", options: ["Digital India, whose 'governance and services on demand' vision area e-Kranti's expanded MMPs directly serve","A national skilling program that is entirely unrelated to any of Digital India's three stated vision areas","A Gujarat-specific state initiative with no bearing whatsoever on other states' programs","An industry-led marketing campaign coordinated mainly by private software vendors"], answer: 0, exp: "e-Kranti's expanded MMPs directly serve Digital India's 'governance and services on demand' vision area." },
  { id: "mmp-50", topic: "itp-mmp", q: "Overall, Central, State and Integrated Mission Mode Projects, built increasingly on shared platforms like the Digitization India Platform, DigiLocker and GI Cloud MeghRaj under e-Kranti, together illustrate:", options: ["A loosely connected set of government IT purchases with no shared design or common standards","A structured national approach where individually owned, milestone-driven e-governance projects increasingly share common digital infrastructure to deliver services more efficiently and consistently","A framework built primarily for private companies, with only incidental relevance to government departments or any Mission Mode Project they might run","An approach that deliberately keeps each project isolated from any form of shared coordination"], answer: 1, exp: "This structured, infrastructure-sharing approach across MMP categories is the central takeaway of NeGP/e-Kranti's design." },

  // ================= E-Governance Policies & Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 (50) =================
  { id: "pol-01", topic: "itp-policy", q: "NeGP's implementation approach, described as 'centralized initiative, decentralized implementation', means:", options: ["Every decision, however small, must be individually approved by a single central office in Delhi, with no delegation permitted to any state government or department, regardless of the department's size or mandate","Overall policy direction and standards are set at the national level, while actual project execution/ownership rests with the concerned ministry, department or state","States are assigned no defined implementation role whatsoever, since NeGP places every function directly under a single central authority's control","Implementation proceeds with no reference to any shared national standard, leaving each department free to design its own separate approach from scratch"], answer: 1, exp: "National-level direction/standards combined with state/department-level execution is the essence of this NeGP principle." },
  { id: "pol-02", topic: "itp-policy", q: "A core NeGP principle regarding project ownership is:", options: ["Only the central government may ever formally hold ownership of any Mission Mode Project, no matter which level actually implements it","A private contracted company always retains full and permanent ownership of any MMP it has been engaged to help deliver","MMPs are jointly owned by the central and state governments concerned, with the implementing ministry/department/state responsible for the project's success","Ownership of every MMP is deliberately left undefined by design, with no ministry, department or state formally accountable for its outcomes, regardless of which government actually funds or runs it"], answer: 2, exp: "NeGP assigns clear ownership to the implementing government body, central and state governments being jointly invested in outcomes." },
  { id: "pol-03", topic: "itp-policy", q: "NeGP explicitly encourages using a public-private partnership (PPP) model:", options: ["Wherever feasible, to leverage private-sector efficiency and investment alongside government oversight in implementing e-governance projects","Never, since NeGP's founding guidelines are understood to rule out any form of private-sector participation in project delivery, even where a state specifically requests such an exception","Only for a narrow set of defense-related projects, where private participation is treated as a special strategic exception","Only on the rare occasions when a state government formally requests it in writing for that one specific project"], answer: 0, exp: "NeGP treats PPP as a viable delivery model wherever it can improve efficiency, as seen in projects like Passport Seva." },
  { id: "pol-04", topic: "itp-policy", q: "The State Wide Area Network (SWAN), part of NeGP's common infrastructure, provides:", options: ["A dedicated national television broadcasting network set up specifically to publicize NeGP's various e-governance achievements, broadcast through Doordarshan's regional state channels","A private social media platform that NeGP contracted to run citizen-awareness and outreach campaigns nationally","A single citizen-facing mobile application that was meant to substitute for any dedicated backbone network layer","Reliable, dedicated network connectivity linking state headquarters down to block/district level offices, supporting e-governance applications"], answer: 3, exp: "SWAN is dedicated network connectivity infrastructure enabling e-governance applications to reach down to the district/block level." },
  { id: "pol-05", topic: "itp-policy", q: "The State Data Centre (SDC), another piece of NeGP's common infrastructure, functions as:", options: ["A centralized facility hosting a state's e-governance applications and data securely, so departments don't each build separate data centres","A citizen-facing document wallet that functions much like DigiLocker's own issued-documents feature, but run at the state level, used mainly to route citizen grievances rather than host applications","A private commercial cloud subscription that is sold directly to individual citizens for their own personal file storage","A physical archive built to store paper files only, with no digital component or any form of online access at all"], answer: 0, exp: "SDCs consolidate hosting for a state's e-governance applications, avoiding duplicated departmental infrastructure." },
  { id: "pol-06", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Common Services Centres (CSC), one of NeGP's common infrastructure components, primarily serve as:", options: ["Data centres that are reserved exclusively for internal meetings and private use by sitting state ministers and staff","Last-mile physical access points (often village-level) through which citizens can access a range of e-governance and other digital services","A private courier delivery network that state departments contract to physically transport paper files between offices, under a long-term logistics contract renewed annually by each state","An online-only service accessible from home, aimed only at citizens who already own a personal computer and connection"], answer: 1, exp: "CSCs are physical last-mile access points, especially important for citizens without personal digital devices/connectivity." },
  { id: "pol-07", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Digital India's three declared vision areas are:", options: ["Only expanding India's electronics-export volumes, treated as the sole vision area with no citizen-facing service component built in","Only cybersecurity, national defense coordination and foreign-policy alignment, with no domestic service-delivery focus mentioned at all","Digital infrastructure as a core utility, governance & services on demand, and digital empowerment of citizens","Only promoting private e-commerce marketplaces, with routine government service delivery left entirely to market forces"], answer: 2, exp: "These three vision areas are the officially stated pillars framing the Digital India program." },
  { id: "pol-08", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Among Digital India's nine pillars is:", options: ["Restricting the rollout of high-speed internet connectivity deliberately to a small set of major metropolitan cities alone","Winding down domestic electronics manufacturing capacity in favour of relying more heavily on imported hardware","Formally banning the personal use of mobile phones inside government offices across every state","Broadband Highways, aimed at providing high-speed digital connectivity across urban and rural India"], answer: 3, exp: "Broadband Highways is one of Digital India's nine pillars, focused on expanding high-speed connectivity nationwide." },
  { id: "pol-09", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Digital India's 'e-Kranti' pillar refers to:", options: ["The electronic delivery of services pillar, expanding Mission Mode Projects and using newer technology (cloud, mobile) for service delivery","A pillar concerned solely with coordinating India's foreign-policy positions and its diplomatic missions abroad","A pillar that specifically prohibits any further expansion of digital-payment channels across government services, coordinated jointly by the Ministry of External Affairs and NIC","A pillar that, unlike the other eight, has no stated connection to any existing Mission Mode Project"], answer: 0, exp: "e-Kranti as a Digital India pillar is specifically about electronic service delivery through an expanded MMP framework." },
  { id: "pol-10", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Digital India's 'IT for Jobs' pillar aims to:", options: ["Phase out IT-related employment altogether in favour of expanding manual clerical roles in government offices","Train youth, particularly in smaller towns/rural areas, in IT skills to improve their employability in the IT/ITeS sector","Benefit only engineering graduates from a small handful of colleges located in a single metropolitan city","Focus exclusively on retraining existing government employees, with no outreach planned toward private-sector job seekers"], answer: 1, exp: "IT for Jobs targets broader IT-skill training, especially reaching beyond major metros, to improve employability." },
  { id: "pol-11", topic: "itp-policy", q: "The National Data Sharing and Accessibility Policy (NDSAP), 2012, primarily encourages:", options: ["Encourage citizens to freely share their own personal data with private companies, with no restriction placed on that sharing","Preserve complete secrecy around all data held by any government department, irrespective of how sensitive that data actually is, a stance the National Data Sharing Policy is said to formally endorse","Government departments to proactively share the non-sensitive data they hold, promoting transparency and enabling data-driven applications","Share government-held datasets exclusively with foreign governments and international agencies, bypassing domestic researchers"], answer: 2, exp: "NDSAP promotes proactive, structured sharing of non-sensitive government data to support transparency and innovation." },
  { id: "pol-12", topic: "itp-policy", q: "India's Open Government Data (OGD) Platform, supported by NDSAP, is best known by which portal?", options: ["A restricted portal reachable only by central government employees who hold a valid official department login","A private company's proprietary analytics dashboard, sold to government departments on an annual subscription basis","A portal that lists only current government job vacancies, without any dataset-publishing function built into it","data.gov.in, which hosts datasets published by various government departments for public and developer access"], answer: 3, exp: "data.gov.in is India's Open Government Data portal, hosting public datasets published under the NDSAP framework." },
  { id: "pol-13", topic: "itp-policy", q: "The Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 is best described as:", options: ["A five-year state government policy setting out objectives and incentives to attract IT/ITeS investment and employment in Gujarat","A central government law that is meant to apply uniformly across every state and union territory in India","A one-time grant scheme that carries no defined policy period and no built-in renewal mechanism of its own","A private industry association's internal charter document, carrying no binding effect on the state government itself, with no reporting obligation to any state department"], answer: 0, exp: "The policy is a defined five-year (2022-27) state government framework of objectives and incentives specific to Gujarat." },
  { id: "pol-14", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Among the stated objectives of Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 is:", options: ["Actively discouraging any company, whether domestic or foreign, from setting up new operations anywhere within Gujarat, steering such investment toward neighbouring states instead","Positioning Gujarat as a preferred destination for IT/ITeS companies, Global Capability Centres, data centres and emerging-technology firms","Restricting IT-investment support strictly to companies that are wholly owned by the state government itself","Removing employment generation entirely as a formally stated objective anywhere within the policy document"], answer: 1, exp: "Investment attraction and positioning Gujarat as a preferred IT/ITeS/GCC/data-centre hub are central policy objectives." },
  { id: "pol-15", topic: "itp-policy", q: "'Capital subsidy', one incentive category under Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27, refers to:", options: ["A special tax that is charged specifically on companies that choose to enter and register within Gujarat","A subsidy that, unusually, is made available only to companies that are legally based outside Gujarat's own borders, intended to offset the compliance cost of relocating from elsewhere in India","Financial support tied to a company's eligible capital investment (e.g. in infrastructure/equipment) when setting up or expanding operations in Gujarat","A one-time waiver of the registration fee, offered on its own with no further financial support attached to it"], answer: 2, exp: "Capital subsidy directly offsets a portion of a company's eligible capital investment, lowering the cost of setting up in Gujarat." },
  { id: "pol-16", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Support for GIDC/IT-park infrastructure, under the Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27, primarily helps companies by:", options: ["Requiring companies to independently build their own approach roads, captive power plants and drainage systems from scratch, within a fixed two-year window set by the state government","Formally barring companies from ever using any existing IT-park facility that GIDC has already constructed","Applying this particular support only to agricultural land-development projects, rather than to any IT park","Providing ready-built industrial/IT-park space and infrastructure so companies don't have to develop land and infrastructure from scratch"], answer: 3, exp: "GIDC/IT-park infrastructure support gives companies ready-built space, avoiding the cost/delay of developing land themselves." },
  { id: "pol-17", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Quality-certification and patent-filing support, offered under the Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27, is aimed at:", options: ["Helping companies offset the cost of obtaining recognized quality certifications and filing patents, encouraging higher-quality, IP-protected innovation","Actively preventing companies from ever filing a fresh patent application while they operate within Gujarat","Supporting only patent filings made in foreign jurisdictions, while explicitly excluding any Indian patent filing, a requirement enforced uniformly regardless of the company's size or sector","Certifying the personal educational qualifications and degrees held by a company's individual staff members"], answer: 0, exp: "This incentive category reduces the cost barrier to obtaining quality certifications and filing patents, encouraging IP-protected innovation." },
  { id: "pol-18", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Skill-development assistance under Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 is intended to:", options: ["Effectively replacing every formal educational institution currently operating within the state of Gujarat","Help companies fund training so the local workforce has the specific skills the IT/ITeS sector needs","Benefit only already highly experienced senior executives who are nearing the end of their careers","Discourage the hiring of locally available talent in favour of recruiting purely from outside the state"], answer: 1, exp: "Skill-development assistance supports training so local talent can meet the specific needs of incoming IT/ITeS investment." },
  { id: "pol-19", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Why does the Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 provide special incentives specifically for data centres and Global Capability Centres (GCCs), distinct from general IT/ITeS incentives?", options: ["Because data centres and GCCs are formally restricted from operating in most other states across India","Because such facilities are generally understood to require very little supporting land or power infrastructure, since most such facilities can reportedly be run from a small rented office space","Because these categories typically involve much larger capital investment and are treated as strategic priorities the state especially wants to attract","Because the policy document only mentions data centres and GCCs briefly, in a single passing footnote"], answer: 2, exp: "Data centres and GCCs typically represent large-scale investment and employment, warranting dedicated, larger incentive categories." },
  { id: "pol-20", topic: "itp-policy", q: "The Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27's dedicated incentives for AI/emerging-technology and cybersecurity companies reflect:", options: ["A restriction that in practice prevents most AI-focused companies from being able to operate anywhere within Gujarat","Incentives that, unusually, apply only to companies that happened to be formally founded before the year 2000","A rule that limits cybersecurity firms strictly to fulfilling government contracts, excluding any private-sector clients, regardless of the size of the contract on offer","A deliberate policy choice to prioritize and attract companies working in high-growth, strategically important technology areas"], answer: 3, exp: "Dedicated AI/emerging-tech and cybersecurity incentives reflect the state's strategic priority on high-growth technology sectors." },
  { id: "pol-21", topic: "itp-policy", q: "'Single-window clearance', offered under Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27, primarily helps investors by:", options: ["Letting them route approvals for land, power, registration and other requirements through one consolidated nodal process instead of multiple separate departments","Effectively removing the need for any government approval whatsoever before an investor can begin operations","Requiring investors to physically travel to and visit every relevant department in person, strictly one at a time, a process critics say actually adds more paperwork than the earlier department-by-department route","Applying this particular benefit only to companies that report having no employees on their payroll at all"], answer: 0, exp: "Single-window clearance consolidates multi-department approvals into one streamlined process, speeding up project setup." },
  { id: "pol-22", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 succeeded which kind of earlier state policy framework?", options: ["There was, as it happens, no earlier Gujarat state IT policy of any kind before this current one was drafted","Earlier Gujarat IT/ITeS policies from previous policy periods, reflecting the state's ongoing, periodically renewed approach to attracting IT investment","A central government policy document that Gujarat's own drafters are said to have reproduced almost word for word","A policy framework that, unusually, was drafted to benefit only a single named private company operating in the state, an arrangement later challenged before the Gujarat High Court"], answer: 1, exp: "Gujarat has periodically renewed its IT/ITeS policy over successive policy periods, with 2022-27 being the current one." },
  { id: "pol-23", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Which best describes the relationship between NeGP and Digital India?", options: ["They are, in fact, entirely unrelated programs that were run independently in two separate countries at different times, despite both programs sharing the same underlying digital-governance goals","NeGP was launched only after Digital India, and is understood to have effectively replaced it back in 2015","Digital India (2015) built on and expanded NeGP's (2006) Mission Mode Project-based approach with a broader three-part vision","Digital India is said to have formally abolished every single Mission Mode Project that NeGP had originally established"], answer: 2, exp: "Digital India built on and broadened NeGP's original MMP-based approach rather than replacing or predating it." },
  { id: "pol-24", topic: "itp-policy", q: "A company deciding whether to set up a Global Capability Centre in Gujarat would most directly consult:", options: ["The Copyright Act 1957, since it is described as the law that governs where companies may physically locate their operations, a claim frequently repeated in older, since-revised textbooks","The RTI Act 2005, since it is described as the specific instrument responsible for approving investment applications","The Aadhaar Act 2016, since it is described as the law that regulates corporate investment decisions directly","The Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27, for the specific incentive categories, eligibility criteria and application process for GCCs"], answer: 3, exp: "The Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 is the specific instrument governing GCC-related incentives and eligibility in the state." },
  { id: "pol-25", topic: "itp-policy", q: "GIFT City (Gujarat International Finance Tec-City) is often cited as an example of a location in Gujarat that particularly benefits from IT/ITeS and financial-sector policy support because it:", options: ["Is a purpose-built business district designed to attract financial services and technology-driven companies, including GCCs, to Gujarat","Is, in practice, a purely residential township where no commercial or business activity of any kind is permitted","Exists today only as a planned name on paper, with essentially no physical development undertaken there so far","Is restricted almost entirely to agricultural land use, with commercial construction largely disallowed on the site, a status that has remained unchanged since the district's original master plan"], answer: 0, exp: "GIFT City is a purpose-built financial/technology business district, a natural beneficiary of IT/ITeS and GCC-focused incentives." },
  { id: "pol-26", topic: "itp-policy", q: "A key difference between the National Data Sharing and Accessibility Policy (NDSAP) and the Digital Personal Data Protection Act (DPDP Act) is that:", options: ["They are, in effect, simply two different official names that both refer to the exact same underlying statute","NDSAP is about government proactively sharing its own non-sensitive data outward; the DPDP Act is about protecting individuals' personal data from misuse — they address different problems","NDSAP is actually the law that protects personal data, while the DPDP Act is the one that shares government-held data outward, with the two roles reversed exactly the wrong way around from how they were actually designed","Neither of these two policy instruments is understood to have any meaningful connection to data of any kind"], answer: 1, exp: "NDSAP addresses government data-sharing/openness; the DPDP Act addresses personal-data protection — distinct concerns." },
  { id: "pol-27", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Digital India's 'Public Internet Access Programme' pillar primarily focuses on:", options: ["Deliberately restricting internet access so that it reaches only large, well-established private corporations first","Formally winding down and discontinuing the further use of Common Services Centres as an access channel altogether","Expanding public access points to the internet, including through Common Services Centres and post offices, especially in underserved areas","Providing internet access strictly to offices of the central government, while pointedly excluding state government offices, leaving state-run CSCs and post offices out of the funding entirely"], answer: 2, exp: "This pillar expands public internet access points, particularly in underserved areas, via channels like CSCs and post offices." },
  { id: "pol-28", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Digital India's 'Information for All' pillar aims to:", options: ["Restricting essentially all government-held information from being accessed at all by members of the general public, a stance that runs directly counter to the transparency goals policymakers describe elsewhere","Applying only to information concerning the day-to-day internal workings of one single named ministry","Requiring that every information request be submitted strictly and only in person at a designated physical office","Make government information and documents easily and openly accessible to citizens online, including through open data and online hosting of information"], answer: 3, exp: "Information for All emphasizes open, online accessibility of government information, complementing transparency policies like NDSAP." },
  { id: "pol-29", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Why does NeGP emphasize 'common infrastructure' (like SWAN, SDC, CSC) shared across Mission Mode Projects rather than each MMP building its own?", options: ["To avoid duplicated cost/effort and ensure MMPs have reliable, consistent, interoperable infrastructure to run on","Because shared common infrastructure is, on balance, understood to offer no real benefit over separate, isolated departmental systems","Because each individual MMP is, under NeGP's own rules, legally barred from ever having infrastructure of its own","Because building out shared infrastructure of this kind is known to make every single MMP noticeably more expensive to run"], answer: 0, exp: "Shared common infrastructure avoids duplicated cost and ensures interoperability across the many MMPs built on it." },
  { id: "pol-30", topic: "itp-policy", q: "The Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27's employment-generation objective is most directly supported by which incentive category?", options: ["Capital subsidy alone, since it is generally treated as having essentially no meaningful link to actual employment outcomes","Skill-development assistance, which helps prepare the local workforce for jobs the incoming IT/ITeS investment creates","Single-window clearance, which mainly speeds up regulatory approvals and carries no real connection to workforce skills","Quality-certification support, which is aimed squarely at product and process standards rather than hiring or new jobs"], answer: 1, exp: "Skill-development assistance most directly supports the employment-generation objective by preparing the local workforce." },
  { id: "pol-31", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Which of these would most likely NOT be covered under Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27's incentive structure?", options: ["A capital subsidy granted specifically for an eligible IT/ITeS company's newly built office premises in the state, approved through the same single-window process used for larger investments","Special incentives extended in support of a large new data-centre investment project being planned in Gujarat","A subsidy for a business entirely unrelated to IT/ITeS, such as a traditional textile mill with no digital component","Skill-development support that is specifically provided for an IT/ITeS company's newly hired local staff members"], answer: 2, exp: "The policy's incentives are targeted at IT/ITeS, data-centre, GCC and related emerging-tech investment, not unrelated traditional industries." },
  { id: "pol-32", topic: "itp-policy", q: "A key rationale behind the Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27's dedicated GCC incentive category is that GCCs:", options: ["Are, as a matter of law, formally barred from operating in any form anywhere across the entire country, a restriction that would apply even to a state actively courting such investment","Have, taken as a category, essentially no meaningful connection to employment generation of any kind","Are automatically ineligible for receiving any kind of state-level incentive anywhere in the country","Represent large-scale, high-value employment and investment opportunities that many states actively compete to attract"], answer: 3, exp: "States actively compete for GCC investment given its scale, which is why the policy carves out dedicated GCC incentives." },
  { id: "pol-33", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Which best summarizes the overall goal of Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27's various incentive categories, taken together?", options: ["To lower the effective cost and complexity of setting up and scaling IT/ITeS operations in Gujarat, making the state more competitive at attracting investment and jobs","To deliberately make Gujarat the single most expensive and administratively complex state in which to launch an IT company, a stated aim that would run directly counter to the policy's own five-year objectives","To wind down IT/ITeS activity within the state entirely over the course of the five-year policy period","To benefit exclusively companies that happen to be headquartered outside India, while excluding domestic Indian firms"], answer: 0, exp: "The policy's incentives collectively aim to lower cost/complexity and make Gujarat more competitive for IT/ITeS investment." },
  { id: "pol-34", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Under NeGP's approach, who is typically accountable for the success of an individual Mission Mode Project?", options: ["No one in particular; Mission Mode Projects are, by design, structured with no clearly defined accountability at all","The specific ministry, department or state government that owns and implements that MMP","Only a private vendor is held accountable in this arrangement, with the government itself bearing no responsibility","Only citizens themselves are held accountable, since they are expected to fully self-manage the project on their own"], answer: 1, exp: "Accountability rests with the owning/implementing ministry, department or state government, consistent with NeGP's ownership principle." },
  { id: "pol-35", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Digital India's 'Electronics Manufacturing' pillar aims to:", options: ["Halt essentially all forms of electronics-manufacturing activity taking place anywhere within Indian territory","Restrict electronics manufacturing strictly to imported components, with domestic production discouraged as a matter of policy","Promote domestic electronics manufacturing, reducing import dependence and building indigenous production capacity","Apply exclusively to software-development activity, with no stated relevance to physical hardware production at all"], answer: 2, exp: "This pillar promotes indigenous electronics production capacity, reducing reliance on imports." },
  { id: "pol-36", topic: "itp-policy", q: "A start-up wants to know if there is a state-level policy specifically incentivizing its plan to open a cybersecurity R&D unit in Gujarat. It should look at:", options: ["The Copyright Act 1957, since it is the general statute that governs how software and creative works are protected","The RTI Act 2005, since it is the general statute that concerns citizens' access to government-held information","The Aadhaar Act 2016, since it is the general statute that concerns identity authentication rather than state investment","The Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27, which includes dedicated incentives for cybersecurity companies"], answer: 3, exp: "The Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 is the appropriate instrument, since it specifically incentivizes cybersecurity companies." },
  { id: "pol-37", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Which statement about NeGP's 'monitoring mechanism' principle is most accurate?", options: ["NeGP envisaged dedicated apex bodies/mechanisms to monitor MMP progress against defined milestones, supporting accountability","NeGP's own founding guidelines are understood to explicitly prohibit any form of ongoing monitoring of MMP progress","Monitoring of MMP progress under NeGP is left entirely to private vendors, with the government itself playing no role in it, with progress reports filed only informally between colleagues","NeGP requires, under its stated rules, that MMP progress be formally reviewed only once every ten years"], answer: 0, exp: "NeGP's design includes monitoring against defined milestones through designated apex/oversight mechanisms." },
  { id: "pol-38", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Which of these is an example of the kind of non-sensitive government data that NDSAP-driven open data platforms like data.gov.in typically publish?", options: ["Individual citizens' private medical treatment records that happen to be held by a particular state health department","Aggregated statistical datasets such as census figures, agricultural production data, or public transport schedules","Classified national-defense intelligence reports that were prepared strictly for internal government use","Individual citizens' personal bank account numbers and login passwords, as recorded by financial institutions"], answer: 1, exp: "Open data platforms publish aggregated, non-sensitive statistical datasets, not private, classified, or credential data." },
  { id: "pol-39", topic: "itp-policy", q: "How does the Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 relate to national programs like Digital India?", options: ["It formally replaces Digital India altogether, within the specific boundaries of the state of Gujarat","It is, in practical terms, understood to have no meaningful relationship at all to Digital India's stated national goals, a claim that overlooks the state's own five-year IT/ITeS policy document entirely","It operates as a complementary state-level policy, directing state investment/incentives in a way that supports the same broad national digital-economy goals","It overrides and, in effect, cancels every piece of central government IT policy applicable within Gujarat"], answer: 2, exp: "State policies like Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy work alongside, and support, the broader national Digital India goals rather than replacing them." },
  { id: "pol-40", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Which pillar of Digital India is most directly reflected in the expansion of Mission Mode Projects and adoption of cloud/mobile-based service delivery discussed under e-Kranti?", options: ["The Electronics Manufacturing pillar, which is centered specifically on hardware production rather than any service","The Broadband Highways pillar, which is concerned mainly with physical last-mile connectivity infrastructure","None of Digital India's nine pillars are understood to relate meaningfully to this particular expansion at all","The e-Kranti pillar itself, which is explicitly about electronic delivery of services"], answer: 3, exp: "The e-Kranti pillar is explicitly the electronic-service-delivery pillar that this expanded MMP framework belongs to." },
  { id: "pol-41", topic: "itp-policy", q: "A defining feature that separates Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 from a purely regulatory law like the IT Act is that the policy:", options: ["Primarily offers incentives/support to encourage desired investment and behavior, rather than imposing legal obligations or penalties","Imposes criminal penalties for any act of non-compliance, in exactly the same manner that the IT Act does","Is, in practical terms, understood to have no meaningful connection whatsoever to investment or incentive design, a claim that overlooks its detailed capital-subsidy and clearance provisions","Is, in fact, itself a national law that applies uniformly across every single state in the country"], answer: 0, exp: "Unlike a regulatory statute, the policy works through incentives to encourage, rather than compel, particular investment behavior." },
  { id: "pol-42", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Which best explains why state-level IT policies (like Gujarat's) exist alongside national programs (like Digital India)?", options: ["States are, as a matter of settled law, legally barred outright from adopting any IT-related policy of their own","States compete to attract investment/jobs within their own borders and can tailor incentives to their specific economic priorities, complementing the national policy framework","National-level programs are said to make every single state-level policy entirely redundant, and therefore unnecessary, a view that overlooks how differently individual states have historically approached IT investment","State-level policies, purely by their design, are said to always directly contradict the national policy framework"], answer: 1, exp: "State policies let individual states compete for investment with tailored incentives, complementing rather than duplicating national policy." },
  { id: "pol-43", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Under Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27, 'quality certification support' would most likely help a company that wants to:", options: ["Avoid ever being formally audited or independently certified by any recognized external body under any circumstance, relying instead purely on its existing reputation within the local market","Register a trademark that protects only its own company logo, and nothing else about its products or processes","Obtain a recognized international quality/security certification (e.g. for its software development processes) to compete for larger contracts","File formally for bankruptcy protection under the insolvency framework that is otherwise applicable to it"], answer: 2, exp: "Quality-certification support helps offset the cost of pursuing recognized certifications that improve a company's competitiveness." },
  { id: "pol-44", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Which of these best illustrates the difference between NeGP's national-level policy role and Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27's state-level role?", options: ["They are, in substance, identical documents that happen to have been issued jointly by the very same authority","NeGP is understood to apply only within Gujarat, while the state policy is understood to govern the rest of India instead, a claim that ignores how differently the two documents define their respective geographic scope, funding source and target beneficiaries","Gujarat's own state policy is said to legally supersede and override NeGP across the whole of the country","NeGP sets a national e-governance framework and Mission Mode Project structure; Gujarat's policy separately incentivizes IT/ITeS investment specifically within the state, operating at a different level and for a different purpose"], answer: 3, exp: "NeGP is a national e-governance framework; Gujarat's policy is a state-level investment-incentive framework — different scope and purpose." },
  { id: "pol-45", topic: "itp-policy", q: "A company that has already received a capital subsidy under Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 for its new office could also separately qualify for:", options: ["Skill-development assistance for training its new hires, since the policy's incentive categories are not mutually exclusive by default","Nothing further at all, since it is understood that only a single incentive may ever be claimed under the entire policy, a limit that is not actually stated anywhere in the policy document itself","A complete exemption from every obligation that is otherwise set out under the IT Act 2000","Automatic issuance of Aadhaar numbers for every one of its employees, arranged directly through the state government"], answer: 0, exp: "The policy's incentive categories generally are not mutually exclusive, so a company may qualify for more than one where eligible." },
  { id: "pol-46", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Which best captures why 'governance and services on demand', a Digital India vision area, is relevant to state IT/ITeS policy as well as e-governance MMPs?", options: ["The two concepts are, in practical terms, entirely unrelated and are understood to share no underlying common goal at all, overlooking how both instruments are ultimately meant to make government and business services easier for ordinary citizens and companies to reach and use","Both aim at the same underlying goal — citizens and businesses accessing services conveniently and efficiently — even though MMPs deliver government services directly and state IT policy attracts the companies/talent that build such digital services","State IT policy is said to fully substitute for, and remove the need for, 'services on demand' altogether","'Services on demand' is described as applying only to private companies, and as having no relevance to government at all"], answer: 1, exp: "MMPs and state investment policy both ultimately serve the same convenient, efficient service-delivery goal, from different angles." },
  { id: "pol-47", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Which of the following would be an appropriate reason for a policy analyst to review both NeGP's founding principles and Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 together?", options: ["Because the two documents are, in fact, understood to be legally identical and fully interchangeable with one another","Because NeGP has, according to this view, since been formally repealed and entirely replaced by Gujarat's own state policy, a claim contradicted by the fact that Gujarat's policy long postdates NeGP's original founding documents","To understand how national e-governance direction (NeGP) and state-level investment incentives (Gujarat's policy) work together to shape IT project outcomes within a state like Gujarat","Because Gujarat's policy is, in this view, described as governing cybercrime prosecution on a nationwide basis"], answer: 2, exp: "Reviewing both together clarifies how national direction and state-level incentives jointly shape a state's actual IT project outcomes." },
  { id: "pol-48", topic: "itp-policy", q: "A foreign IT company evaluating whether to expand into India, and specifically into Gujarat, would most likely consult national policy (NeGP/Digital India) for:", options: ["Nothing useful at all, since national policy is treated here as being irrelevant to any private company's own decision, a view that ignores how heavily such decisions typically rely on published government policy documents","Only NeGP, since state-level policy is said in this view to have no bearing on private investment decisions at all","Only Gujarat's own state policy, since national policy is claimed here to carry no relevance to the state","Broad national e-governance/digital-economy direction and priorities, and Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 for the specific state-level incentives and approval process"], answer: 3, exp: "A company would look at national policy for overall direction and Gujarat's policy for the specific, actionable state-level incentives." },
  { id: "pol-49", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Which best describes the overall purpose of e-governance policy instruments like NeGP, Digital India, NDSAP and Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27, taken together?", options: ["They collectively set direction, priorities, transparency norms and investment incentives that shape how e-governance and IT projects are planned, funded and delivered across national and state levels","They form, in this view, a random and disconnected collection of documents that share no underlying common purpose","They are described in this view as existing solely to regulate cybercrime, with no other purpose stated anywhere","They apply, in this view, only to private companies, and are said to carry no relevance to government projects at all, a claim that overlooks how heavily private IT/ITeS investment in Gujarat depends on exactly this kind of government policy support"], answer: 0, exp: "Together these instruments set the direction, transparency norms and incentives shaping e-governance and IT project delivery nationwide and in the states." },
  { id: "pol-50", topic: "itp-policy", q: "Overall, the policy landscape spanning NeGP's founding principles, Digital India's vision, supporting policies like NDSAP, and state-level frameworks like Gujarat's IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27 illustrates:", options: ["A single, monolithic policy document that, in this view, draws no distinction at all between national and state roles, a description that overlooks how differently NeGP's national guidelines and Gujarat's own five-year incentive structure are actually organised and applied","A layered approach where national policy sets broad direction and transparency norms while state policy adds targeted, competitive incentives to translate that direction into actual local investment and IT projects","A policy framework that, in this view, has no practical connection at all to real IT/ITeS investment decisions","An approach that is described, in this view, as deliberately discouraging any form of state-level policy innovation"], answer: 1, exp: "This layered national-direction-plus-state-incentive structure is the central takeaway of India's e-governance/IT policy landscape." },

  // ================= IT Organizations & Gujarat STI Policy (50) =================
  { id: "org-01", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "MeitY (Ministry of Electronics & Information Technology) functions as:", options: ["An industry association representing private IT companies before the union government, though it holds no formal government authority of its own","The union government's nodal ministry for IT policy, e-governance programs and electronics manufacturing","A state-level agency operating only within the boundaries of Gujarat","A private testing laboratory that carries no formal government authority at all"], answer: 1, exp: "MeitY is the union nodal ministry driving national IT policy, e-governance programs and electronics manufacturing initiatives." },
  { id: "org-02", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "MeitY was earlier known, before being elevated to a full ministry, as:", options: ["The Ministry of Home Affairs, functioning as the ministry's parent body before 2016","The Reserve Bank of India, functioning as the ministry's regulatory parent and advising on IT-financing matters before its 2016 reorganization","The Department of Electronics and Information Technology (DeitY), functioning under a different ministry before its 2016 reorganization","NASSCOM, functioning as its private-sector predecessor body before 2016"], answer: 2, exp: "MeitY's predecessor was the Department of Electronics and Information Technology (DeitY) before being elevated to a full ministry." },
  { id: "org-03", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "The National Informatics Centre (NIC) primarily:", options: ["Only manufactures computer hardware for export to other countries, a function that in fact belongs to private electronics manufacturers instead","Regulates private telecom companies' licensing and spectrum allocation across India","Issues Aadhaar numbers to citizens on behalf of the union government's identity program","Designs, builds and operates the IT infrastructure and network (including NICNET) that ministries, departments and states use to run their e-governance applications"], answer: 3, exp: "NIC's core role is providing the operational IT backbone/infrastructure for government e-governance systems." },
  { id: "org-04", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "NIC's network backbone connecting government offices nationwide is commonly referred to as:", options: ["NICNET","SWAYAM, the online education platform","MeghRaj, the cloud strategy","DigiLocker, the document wallet"], answer: 0, exp: "NICNET is NIC's government network infrastructure, distinct from citizen-facing platforms like DigiLocker or SWAYAM." },
  { id: "org-05", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "C-DAC (Centre for Development of Advanced Computing) is primarily known for:", options: ["Issuing digital signature certificates as one of India's licensed Certifying Authorities, a role that is in fact carried out by a separately licensed private-sector body","Research and development in advanced computing, including India's indigenous PARAM series of supercomputers, language technology, and cybersecurity","Operating India's internet exchange points and managing the '.in' domain registry","Administering the Aadhaar database on behalf of the statutory authority concerned"], answer: 1, exp: "C-DAC's mandate centers on advanced-computing R&D, including HPC (PARAM), language technology and cybersecurity research." },
  { id: "org-06", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "C-DAC's development of the PARAM series of supercomputers is historically significant because it:", options: ["Was purchased entirely from a foreign vendor, with no domestic development work involved, a claim that overlooks the extensive domestic engineering work C-DAC itself carried out","Has, in fact, no meaningful connection to computing research of any kind","Demonstrated India's ability to indigenously build high-performance computing capability after international export restrictions on supercomputer technology","Was a project run entirely by a private telecom company with no C-DAC involvement"], answer: 2, exp: "PARAM demonstrated India's ability to build supercomputing capability indigenously after facing export restrictions abroad." },
  { id: "org-07", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "STQC (Standardisation Testing and Quality Certification) primarily provides:", options: ["Aadhaar enrollment services delivered directly to citizens at designated centres, a function that is in fact carried out separately by UIDAI's own enrollment network","Cybersecurity incident response coordination for government and private networks","Domain name registration services specifically for '.in' websites nationwide","Independent testing and certification services for IT systems/products, verifying quality, security and interoperability, often before government deployment"], answer: 3, exp: "STQC's core role is independent testing and quality/security certification of IT systems, typically before deployment." },
  { id: "org-08", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which body is responsible for issuing and managing the Aadhaar number under statutory authority?", options: ["UIDAI (Unique Identification Authority of India)","NIC (National Informatics Centre)","STQC (Standardisation Testing and Quality Certification)","NASSCOM (National Association of Software and Service Companies)"], answer: 0, exp: "UIDAI is the statutory authority responsible for issuing and managing Aadhaar numbers." },
  { id: "org-09", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "UIDAI's statutory authority to issue Aadhaar numbers was formalized through:", options: ["The IT Act, 2000, exclusively, with no separate identity-specific statute ever enacted","The Aadhaar Act, 2016, which placed UIDAI on a statutory footing after it had earlier operated under an executive notification","The Copyright Act, 1957, which covers literary and artistic works and has no bearing on statutory identity authorities like UIDAI","An informal administrative understanding that carried no defined legal basis at the time"], answer: 1, exp: "UIDAI operated under executive notification before the Aadhaar Act, 2016 gave it formal statutory authority." },
  { id: "org-10", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "CERT-In (Indian Computer Emergency Response Team) serves as India's:", options: ["National agency for issuing passports through its network of Passport Seva Kendras","National agency for corporate registration and statutory filing compliance matters","National nodal agency for responding to cybersecurity incidents","National agency for land record digitization under individual state revenue departments"], answer: 2, exp: "CERT-In is India's national nodal agency for cybersecurity incident response, detailed further in the Cyber Security unit." },
  { id: "org-11", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which of the following best distinguishes STQC from CERT-In?", options: ["They are simply two different official names used for the exact same organization","STQC handles incident response and CERT-In handles pre-deployment testing — the reverse of their actual roles, a description that inverts the roles both organizations actually perform in practice","Neither organization is understood to have any defined role in IT security at all","STQC focuses on testing/certifying IT systems for quality and security before deployment, while CERT-In focuses on responding to cybersecurity incidents after they occur"], answer: 3, exp: "STQC's role is pre-deployment testing/certification; CERT-In's role is post-incident cybersecurity response — complementary, not identical." },
  { id: "org-12", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "NIXI (National Internet Exchange of India) is primarily responsible for:", options: ["Operating internet exchange points to keep domestic internet traffic within India, and managing the '.in' domain registry","Issuing digital signature certificates through a network of licensed Certifying Authorities, a role that is in fact carried out separately by the Controller of Certifying Authorities","Testing government software for quality before it is approved for deployment","Administering the Aadhaar Act on behalf of the union government's identity program"], answer: 0, exp: "NIXI operates internet exchange points and manages the '.in' domain registry, keeping domestic traffic within India." },
  { id: "org-13", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Digital India Corporation (formerly Media Lab Asia) functions as:", options: ["A private venture-capital fund that carries no formal affiliation with government at all, despite operating with recurring government grant funding routed through MeitY","A Section 8 (not-for-profit) company under MeitY that drives specific Digital India programs and digital innovation initiatives","A state government department located specifically within Gujarat's administration","The certifying authority responsible for issuing Aadhaar numbers to citizens nationwide"], answer: 1, exp: "Digital India Corporation is a not-for-profit company under MeitY driving specific Digital India programs and innovation efforts." },
  { id: "org-14", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "NASSCOM (National Association of Software and Service Companies) is best classified as:", options: ["A ministry of the union government with its own cabinet-rank minister","A statutory regulator holding legal powers to certify government IT systems, a power that in fact belongs to bodies like STQC rather than to NASSCOM itself","An industry association representing private IT/software companies, not a government body","A Gujarat state government department reporting to the state's IT secretary"], answer: 2, exp: "NASSCOM is a private-sector industry association, not a government ministry, regulator or department." },
  { id: "org-15", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which of the following is a government body, as opposed to a private industry association?", options: ["NASSCOM, the national private-sector software industry association","GESIA, Gujarat's own private IT/ITeS industry association","A private IT company operating without any government affiliation or oversight","NIC (National Informatics Centre)"], answer: 3, exp: "NIC is a government body under MeitY; NASSCOM and GESIA are industry associations, not government bodies." },
  { id: "org-16", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Gujarat Informatics Limited (GIL) functions as:", options: ["Gujarat's nodal state agency for IT-enabled services, implementing e-governance projects and infrastructure for the state — the state counterpart of NIC","A private company operating with no state government ownership or oversight of any kind","The union government's dedicated cybersecurity incident-response agency for all states","An industry body representing private IT companies operating specifically within Gujarat, distinct from the state-owned agency that actually implements Gujarat's own e-governance projects"], answer: 0, exp: "GIL is Gujarat's own nodal IT-services agency, serving a role for the state analogous to NIC's role at the union level." },
  { id: "org-17", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Within Gujarat's state government structure, IT/S&T policy-making is primarily overseen by:", options: ["The Ministry of External Affairs, which oversees passport and consular matters instead","The Department of Science & Technology, Gujarat","NASSCOM, the national private-sector software industry body, not a state department","CERT-In, the national cybersecurity incident-response agency, not a policy department"], answer: 1, exp: "The Department of Science & Technology, Gujarat is the state department overseeing IT/S&T policy for the state." },
  { id: "org-18", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "GESIA is best described as:", options: ["A Gujarat state government department reporting directly to the Chief Secretary","The Gujarat counterpart of UIDAI, issuing identity numbers to state residents","An industry association representing Gujarat's IT/ITeS companies, distinct from any government body","A certifying authority licensed to issue digital signature certificates in Gujarat, a role that in fact belongs to licensed Certifying Authorities operating under the IT Act"], answer: 2, exp: "GESIA (Gujarat Electronics and Software Industries Association) is a private industry association, not a government department or authority." },
  { id: "org-19", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which best distinguishes GIL from GESIA?", options: ["They are simply two different names used for referring to the same single organization","GESIA is a government agency and GIL is a private association — the reverse of their actual roles, a description that inverts which of the two bodies is actually the government agency","Neither organization is understood to have any connection to Gujarat's IT sector at all","GIL is a Gujarat state government agency implementing e-governance projects; GESIA is a private industry association representing IT/ITeS companies — one is government, the other is not"], answer: 3, exp: "GIL is a government agency; GESIA is a private industry association — a fundamental distinction in their nature and role." },
  { id: "org-20", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Among the stated objectives of Gujarat's Science, Technology and Innovation (STI) Policy is:", options: ["Strengthening the state's R&D ecosystem and promoting startups, innovation and industry-academia linkages","Actively discouraging any research and development activity from taking place in the state, a stance that would run directly counter to the policy's own stated innovation objectives","Restricting universities from being permitted to collaborate with industry partners","Eliminating all forms of state funding directed toward startups and incubators"], answer: 0, exp: "The STI Policy's core objectives include strengthening R&D, encouraging startups/innovation and deepening industry-academia linkages." },
  { id: "org-21", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Gujarat's STI Policy places particular emphasis on:", options: ["Banning the continued use of any technology that was first developed after the year 2000","Emerging-technology focus areas, encouraging research and enterprise activity in fields expected to shape future economic growth","Restricting research funding so that it flows only toward agricultural topics specifically, excluding the emerging-technology and enterprise focus areas the policy actually names","Eliminating industry-academia collaboration entirely as a matter of stated policy"], answer: 1, exp: "The STI Policy prioritizes emerging-technology focus areas as part of its innovation and R&D strategy." },
  { id: "org-22", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Gujarat's Student Startup and Innovation Policy (SSIP) reflects the broader STI ecosystem's goal of:", options: ["Preventing students from being permitted to pursue any entrepreneurial activity at all, a stance that would run directly counter to SSIP's own stated founding objectives","Only supporting startups that happen to have been founded outside Gujarat's borders","Encouraging and supporting student-led innovation and entrepreneurship, often through funding, mentorship and incubation support","Replacing the need for any formal university education for aspiring entrepreneurs"], answer: 2, exp: "SSIP supports student-led innovation and entrepreneurship through funding, mentorship and incubation, aligned with the STI ecosystem's goals." },
  { id: "org-23", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Industry-academia linkage, a goal under Gujarat's STI Policy, is meant to:", options: ["Keeping universities and industry completely separate, with no interaction encouraged, an approach that would run directly counter to this linkage goal's own stated purpose","Applying this particular linkage goal only to a single named university in the state","Preventing any research funding whatsoever from ever reaching university departments","Connect universities/research institutions with industry so research outcomes translate more effectively into real-world products, services and startups"], answer: 3, exp: "Industry-academia linkage aims to translate research more effectively into practical, real-world outcomes." },
  { id: "org-24", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which best distinguishes the Gujarat STI Policy from the Gujarat IT/ITeS Policy 2022-27?", options: ["The STI Policy focuses on strengthening R&D, innovation and the startup ecosystem broadly (across sectors), while the IT/ITeS Policy specifically incentivizes IT/ITeS investment, employment and infrastructure","They are simply two different names used for the exact same underlying policy document, issued under one file number","The STI Policy is said to cover only IT/ITeS companies, making its scope identical to the IT/ITeS Policy in every respect, a claim that overlooks the STI Policy's much broader, cross-sector R&D mandate","Neither policy is understood to have any meaningful connection to Gujarat's broader economy at all"], answer: 0, exp: "The STI Policy has a broad, cross-sector R&D/innovation focus, while the IT/ITeS Policy specifically targets IT/ITeS investment and jobs." },
  { id: "org-25", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "A government department planning to launch a new e-governance application in Gujarat would typically approach GIL for:", options: ["Cybercrime investigation and prosecution, since that is described as GIL's core statutory function, a function that in fact falls under CERT-In's nodal incident-response mandate instead","Implementation support, hosting and IT infrastructure for the application, given GIL's role as the state's nodal e-governance agency","Issuing Aadhaar numbers directly to citizens applying through the new application","Drafting national IT legislation on behalf of the union government's law ministry"], answer: 1, exp: "GIL provides implementation support and infrastructure for Gujarat's e-governance projects as the state's nodal IT agency." },
  { id: "org-26", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Before a new government IT application goes live, which organization would most likely be approached to independently test and certify its quality/security?", options: ["NASSCOM, the national private software industry body","GESIA, Gujarat's private IT/ITeS association","STQC","Digital India Corporation, the MeitY innovation arm"], answer: 2, exp: "STQC is the organization mandated to independently test and certify IT systems' quality and security before deployment." },
  { id: "org-27", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "If a government department in Gujarat suffers a cybersecurity incident (e.g. a data breach), which national body would be the appropriate nodal point of contact for incident response coordination?", options: ["STQC, the testing and certification body","NASSCOM, the industry association","NIXI, the domain-registry operator","CERT-In"], answer: 3, exp: "CERT-In is India's national nodal agency specifically responsible for coordinating cybersecurity incident response." },
  { id: "org-28", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which organization operates internet exchange points and manages India's '.in' domain registry?", options: ["NIXI","C-DAC, the R&D body","UIDAI, the Aadhaar authority","GIL, Gujarat's IT agency"], answer: 0, exp: "NIXI is specifically responsible for internet exchange point operation and the '.in' domain registry." },
  { id: "org-29", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which of the following best fits the description 'a Section 8 not-for-profit company under MeitY driving specific Digital India programs and innovation initiatives'?", options: ["NASSCOM, the private national software industry association","Digital India Corporation","GESIA, Gujarat's own private IT/ITeS industry association","UIDAI, the statutory Aadhaar-issuing authority"], answer: 1, exp: "Digital India Corporation is the Section 8 not-for-profit company under MeitY that fits this description." },
  { id: "org-30", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "A private software company wanting to network with peers, advocate for favorable IT policy, or benchmark industry practices would most likely turn to:", options: ["UIDAI, since it is described here as the body regulating all private companies","STQC, since it is described here as the organization handling industry networking events","An industry association such as NASSCOM (nationally) or GESIA (in Gujarat)","CERT-In, since it is described here as the agency that manages business associations"], answer: 2, exp: "Industry associations like NASSCOM and GESIA are the appropriate bodies for networking, advocacy and industry benchmarking." },
  { id: "org-31", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which best explains why C-DAC and NIC, though both government IT bodies, are not interchangeable?", options: ["They are simply two different names used for referring to the exact same organization","C-DAC runs government applications while NIC does pure research — the reverse of their actual roles, a description that inverts which of the two bodies actually performs research versus operations","Neither organization is understood to be involved in any government IT function at all","C-DAC's core mandate is advanced-computing research and development, while NIC's core mandate is operating IT infrastructure/services that run government applications"], answer: 3, exp: "C-DAC focuses on R&D, while NIC focuses on operational IT infrastructure/services — distinct, complementary mandates." },
  { id: "org-32", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "A state's e-governance project relying on Aadhaar-based authentication for beneficiary verification would primarily interface with:", options: ["UIDAI's authentication APIs","NIXI's domain-name registry system","GESIA's private member directory","C-DAC's supercomputing cluster"], answer: 0, exp: "Aadhaar-based authentication is performed through UIDAI's authentication infrastructure/APIs, not the other listed bodies." },
  { id: "org-33", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which best describes the overall relationship between MeitY and bodies like NIC, C-DAC, STQC and UIDAI?", options: ["They are entirely independent bodies that carry no defined policy relationship to MeitY whatsoever","MeitY is the nodal ministry setting overall IT policy direction, while NIC, C-DAC, STQC and UIDAI are specialized bodies/agencies that operate under or alongside it (or, for UIDAI, under its own statute) to implement specific functions","NIC, C-DAC, STQC and UIDAI are described here as collectively setting the policy that MeitY itself must then follow, reversing the actual reporting relationship these specialized bodies have with the nodal ministry","MeitY is described here as a private company that carries no government authority of its own"], answer: 1, exp: "MeitY sets overall policy direction, while these specialized bodies implement distinct functions under or alongside it." },
  { id: "org-34", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Gujarat's counterpart to the union government's NIC, in terms of implementing state e-governance infrastructure, is:", options: ["GESIA, Gujarat's private industry association for IT/ITeS companies","Department of Science & Technology, Gujarat","GIL (Gujarat Informatics Limited)","NASSCOM, the national private software industry body"], answer: 2, exp: "GIL is Gujarat's implementation-focused nodal e-governance agency, functionally analogous to NIC at the union level." },
  { id: "org-35", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which of these government/industry bodies would be inappropriate to approach for help drafting or reviewing a company's own internal cybersecurity policy — because it is not a cybersecurity-focused organization?", options: ["CERT-In, which handles cybersecurity incident response and advisories nationally, making it one of the more natural bodies to consult on cybersecurity policy questions","STQC, which can independently test and certify the security of IT systems","C-DAC, which carries recognized cybersecurity research and development expertise","NIXI, whose mandate is internet exchange operation and domain registry management, not cybersecurity advisory"], answer: 3, exp: "NIXI's mandate is internet exchange/domain registry operation, not cybersecurity advisory, unlike CERT-In, STQC or C-DAC." },
  { id: "org-36", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which of these organizations was established specifically to provide India's IT backbone for running government e-governance applications, well before Digital India or NeGP existed?", options: ["NIC, established in 1976, predating most later e-governance programs","UIDAI, established only after the Aadhaar Act was passed in 2016","Digital India Corporation, established specifically as part of Digital India in 2015","GESIA, a private industry body that carries no formal government role of its own"], answer: 0, exp: "NIC's establishment in 1976 predates NeGP (2006) and Digital India (2015) by decades." },
  { id: "org-37", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which best explains why NASSCOM and GESIA, despite not being government bodies, are still relevant to India's/Gujarat's IT ecosystem?", options: ["They are said to hold full statutory authority allowing them to issue government regulations, a power that in fact remains with statutory government bodies rather than industry associations","As industry associations, they represent private-sector interests, facilitate networking/advocacy, and often collaborate with government on skilling, policy feedback and industry events","They are described here as replacing the need for any dedicated government IT body","They are understood to carry no meaningful relevance to the IT ecosystem at all"], answer: 1, exp: "Industry associations play a meaningful role through advocacy, networking and government collaboration, even without statutory authority." },
  { id: "org-38", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Under the Gujarat STI Policy, funding/support directed toward 'emerging-technology focus areas' would most likely apply to a startup working on:", options: ["A traditional handwritten ledger-keeping service that carries no technology component at all, falling well outside the technology-driven focus areas the STI Policy is designed to support","A business that is explicitly excluded from receiving any state innovation support","An artificial intelligence-based diagnostic tool, since AI is typically treated as an emerging-technology priority area","An activity that is entirely unrelated to research, technology or innovation of any kind"], answer: 2, exp: "AI-based innovation typically falls squarely within the kind of emerging-technology focus areas the STI Policy prioritizes." },
  { id: "org-39", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which best captures the difference in scope between MeitY and GIL?", options: ["They are described here as holding identical, fully overlapping national jurisdiction","GIL is said to hold authority over the whole of India, while MeitY is limited only to Gujarat, a claim that reverses which of the two bodies actually holds the national-level mandate","Neither organization is understood to have any clearly defined jurisdiction at all","MeitY is the union government's nodal IT ministry with national jurisdiction; GIL is a Gujarat state government agency with jurisdiction limited to implementing IT/e-governance for that state"], answer: 3, exp: "MeitY operates at the national level, while GIL's jurisdiction is limited to implementing IT/e-governance within Gujarat." },
  { id: "org-40", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which of these best represents a 'quality assurance/testing' function among India's IT organizations?", options: ["STQC testing and certifying a new e-governance application before it is deployed","NASSCOM hosting an industry networking event for its member companies","NIXI processing the registration of a newly requested '.in' domain name, a routine registration task rather than an independent quality or security check","GESIA publishing its regular industry newsletter for member companies"], answer: 0, exp: "STQC's testing/certification of a new application before deployment is the clearest example of a quality-assurance function here." },
  { id: "org-41", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "A researcher wanting to collaborate on India's high-performance computing (supercomputing) capability would most likely approach:", options: ["NIXI, the internet-exchange and domain-registry operator","C-DAC","GESIA, Gujarat's private IT/ITeS association","CERT-In, the incident-response agency"], answer: 1, exp: "C-DAC's core mandate includes high-performance computing R&D, including the PARAM supercomputer series." },
  { id: "org-42", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which best describes the overall structure of union-level IT organizations described in this topic?", options: ["All these organizations are said to perform an identical, fully interchangeable function","None of these organizations is understood to report to or coordinate with MeitY in any way at all, despite MeitY's well-documented role as the nodal ministry overseeing each of them","A nodal ministry (MeitY) sets policy direction, while specialized bodies beneath or alongside it (NIC, C-DAC, STQC, UIDAI, CERT-In, NIXI, Digital India Corporation) each handle a distinct function — infrastructure, R&D, certification, identity, incident response, internet governance, and innovation programs respectively","Only NASSCOM is described here as overseeing the work of all of these government bodies"], answer: 2, exp: "This layered structure — nodal ministry plus specialized bodies with distinct functions — accurately reflects how union IT organizations relate to MeitY." },
  { id: "org-43", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which best describes the overall structure of Gujarat's state-level IT ecosystem described in this topic?", options: ["GIL and GESIA are simply the same organization operating under two different official names","The Department of Science & Technology is said to play no role at all in Gujarat's IT ecosystem, despite this department being the one that formally sets Gujarat's IT/S&T policy direction","GESIA is described here as a government department holding statutory implementation authority","A state policy department (Science & Technology) sets direction, GIL implements state e-governance projects and infrastructure, and GESIA represents the private IT/ITeS industry — government and industry bodies working alongside, not in place of, each other"], answer: 3, exp: "This layered structure — policy department, implementation agency, and industry association — accurately reflects Gujarat's IT ecosystem." },
  { id: "org-44", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which body would a Gujarat-based IT startup most likely approach for support/funding specifically framed around research, innovation and industry-academia collaboration, as opposed to IT/ITeS investment incentives generally?", options: ["Programs under the Gujarat STI Policy","CERT-In, since it is described here as directly funding IT startups","NIXI, since it is described here as managing startup funding programs","The Copyright Act, since it is described here as directly funding startups too"], answer: 0, exp: "The STI Policy is specifically framed around R&D, innovation and industry-academia collaboration, unlike the other listed options." },
  { id: "org-45", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "A key reason GPSC-style exams distinguish between 'government bodies' (MeitY, NIC, UIDAI, GIL) and 'industry associations' (NASSCOM, GESIA) is that:", options: ["There is understood to be no meaningful distinction between the two categories at all","Government bodies have statutory/administrative authority and public accountability, while industry associations represent private-sector interests without government regulatory power","Industry associations are said to always hold greater legal authority than government bodies","Government bodies are described here as always being privately funded, just like industry associations, a claim that overlooks how differently these two categories of body are actually financed"], answer: 1, exp: "Government bodies carry statutory/administrative authority and public accountability, distinct from private industry associations." },
  { id: "org-46", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which of these correctly pairs an organization with its primary function?", options: ["NIXI — issuing Aadhaar numbers to registered citizens nationwide","C-DAC — operating internet exchange points and the domain registry","STQC — quality testing and certification of IT systems","UIDAI — supercomputing research and PARAM development"], answer: 2, exp: "STQC's primary function is quality testing and certification of IT systems; the other pairings in this question are swapped incorrectly." },
  { id: "org-47", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which of these correctly pairs an organization with its primary function?", options: ["GESIA — issuing digital signature certificates as a licensed Certifying Authority, a certifying function that in fact belongs to licensed Certifying Authorities, not industry bodies","CERT-In — running the government's shared cloud hosting infrastructure directly","NASSCOM — statutory regulation and licensing of all IT companies nationwide","NIC — providing IT infrastructure/services that run government e-governance applications"], answer: 3, exp: "NIC's primary function is providing the IT infrastructure/services that run government e-governance applications." },
  { id: "org-48", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which of these correctly pairs a Gujarat body with its primary role?", options: ["GIL — implementing e-governance projects and infrastructure for the Gujarat state government","GESIA — described here as a Gujarat state government ministry with cabinet rank","Department of Science & Technology, Gujarat — described here as a private industry association","GIL — described here as a national-level ministry holding authority over all states"], answer: 0, exp: "GIL's primary role is implementing e-governance projects/infrastructure for Gujarat; the other pairings here mislabel each body's nature." },
  { id: "org-49", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Overall, MeitY, NIC, C-DAC, STQC, UIDAI, CERT-In, NIXI and Digital India Corporation at the union level, alongside GIL, the Department of Science & Technology and GESIA in Gujarat, together illustrate:", options: ["A single monolithic organization that is said to draw no functional distinction between any of its bodies","A layered ecosystem of specialized government bodies (policy, infrastructure, R&D, certification, identity, security, internet governance) and industry associations working at both national and state levels to plan, build, certify and support India's IT projects","A purely private-sector ecosystem that is described here as involving no government participation at all","An ecosystem said to be limited entirely to a single state, with no national-level bodies involved anywhere, a description that overlooks the Gujarat-level bodies discussed earlier in this same topic"], answer: 1, exp: "This layered ecosystem of specialized government bodies and industry associations, spanning national and state levels, is the central takeaway." },
  { id: "org-50", topic: "itp-orgs", q: "Which best summarizes why understanding the distinct roles of these organizations matters for someone studying IT projects and governance?", options: ["These roles are described here as having no practical relevance to real e-governance projects at all","Only one of these many organizations is ever described here as being relevant in practice","Knowing which organization is responsible for which function (policy, infrastructure, R&D, certification, identity, incident response, industry representation) clarifies who to approach, what to expect, and how accountability is structured in a real e-governance project","These organizations' roles are described here as entirely interchangeable, with no meaningful distinction between them, a claim that overlooks how differently each body's function actually determines who should be approached"], answer: 2, exp: "Clear knowledge of each organization's function clarifies accountability and the right point of contact in real e-governance projects." },

  // ================= Legislative Framework for IT in India (50) =================
  { id: "leg-01", topic: "itp-legal", q: "The IT Act, 2000 primarily gives legal recognition to:", options: ["Only handwritten paper signatures, with electronic records and signatures excluded from any legal recognition under the Act, a position the Act's drafters explicitly rejected when framing its core provisions","Electronic records and digital signatures, so an electronic document/signature can carry the same legal weight as its paper counterpart","Criminal defamation offences exclusively, with no bearing on civil or commercial electronic transactions","Trademark registration for company logos, a matter that instead falls under separate intellectual-property legislation"], answer: 1, exp: "The IT Act's foundational civil/commercial contribution is legal recognition of electronic records and digital signatures." },
  { id: "leg-02", topic: "itp-legal", q: "The IT Act, 2000 was substantially modeled on:", options: ["The Indian Penal Code exclusively, with no reference made to any international commercial-law template, a claim that overlooks how heavily the Act's drafters actually borrowed from abroad","A purely domestic framework drafted with no reference to any international model or convention at all","The UNCITRAL Model Law on Electronic Commerce, an internationally recognized template for e-commerce/electronic-transaction legislation","The Aadhaar Act, which in fact came many years later and addresses an entirely different subject"], answer: 2, exp: "The IT Act drew heavily on the UNCITRAL Model Law on Electronic Commerce, a widely referenced international template." },
  { id: "leg-03", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Under the IT Act, 2000, a valid electronic contract (e-contract) is best understood as:", options: ["A contract that is automatically treated as invalid simply because it was never signed on paper","A type of contract recognized only when a government official is a party to the agreement","A type of contract that, unusually, requires no consideration or mutual agreement between the parties, a description that misstates the ordinary contract-law requirements the Act still preserves","An agreement formed and concluded through electronic means, which the Act treats as legally valid provided the general requirements of a valid contract are met"], answer: 3, exp: "The IT Act enables e-contracts formed through electronic means to be legally valid, provided ordinary contract requirements are satisfied." },
  { id: "leg-04", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Section 79 of the IT Act, 2000, dealing with intermediary due diligence, generally provides that:", options: ["An online intermediary (like a platform hosting user content) is not liable for third-party content if it observes the due-diligence obligations prescribed under the Act/rules","Every intermediary is automatically held liable for all user-generated content, with no exceptions carved out anywhere, a reading that ignores the conditional safe-harbor language Section 79 actually uses","Intermediaries carry no obligations whatsoever, no matter what kind of content they end up hosting","Only platforms that are themselves owned by the government are recognized as intermediaries under this section"], answer: 0, exp: "Section 79 provides a conditional safe harbor for intermediaries that meet due-diligence obligations, not a blanket exemption or blanket liability." },
  { id: "leg-05", topic: "itp-legal", q: "The Controller of Certifying Authorities (CCA), established under the IT Act, 2000, is responsible for:", options: ["Issuing Aadhaar numbers directly to citizens, a function that instead sits with UIDAI under its own separate statutory framework established in 2016","Regulating and licensing Certifying Authorities that issue digital signature certificates, forming the backbone of India's digital-signature trust infrastructure","Prosecuting cybercrime cases directly before criminal courts across the country","Registering copyrights for software, a function that instead falls under the Copyright Office"], answer: 1, exp: "The CCA regulates/licenses Certifying Authorities, underpinning the trust infrastructure for India's digital signature system." },
  { id: "leg-06", topic: "itp-legal", q: "The IT (Amendment) Act, 2008 broadened the IT Act's original 'digital signature' concept into the more technology-neutral term:", options: ["'Handwritten signature', a term that in fact reverses the shift the amendment made toward electronic authentication","'Biometric password', a term that does not, in fact, appear anywhere in the amended Act's text","'Electronic signature', accommodating authentication technologies beyond a single specific digital-signature method","'Aadhaar signature', a term that would tie all authentication exclusively to the Aadhaar system alone"], answer: 2, exp: "The 2008 amendment introduced the broader, technology-neutral term 'electronic signature' alongside 'digital signature'." },
  { id: "leg-07", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which best describes the civil/commercial focus of the IT Act, 2000 as distinct from its cybercrime provisions (covered separately under the Cyber Security unit)?", options: ["The civil/commercial provisions instead define criminal penalties for offences like hacking and identity theft","The civil/commercial provisions are, in substance, identical in content to the Act's separate cybercrime provisions, a claim that ignores how differently the two sets of provisions are structured and enforced","The IT Act is generally understood to contain no civil or commercial provisions of its own at all","The civil/commercial provisions establish the legal validity of electronic records, digital/electronic signatures, e-contracts, and the regulatory structure for Certifying Authorities — the foundation for lawful digital transactions"], answer: 3, exp: "The IT Act's civil/commercial provisions establish the legal foundation for digital transactions, distinct from its separately-covered cybercrime sections." },
  { id: "leg-08", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Digital signature certificates in India are issued by:", options: ["Licensed Certifying Authorities, operating under the Controller of Certifying Authorities' regulatory oversight established by the IT Act, 2000","Any private individual, issued freely with no licensing requirement or regulatory oversight of any kind","UIDAI exclusively, since digital signature certificates are treated as part of Aadhaar's identity infrastructure","The Copyright Office, which is generally understood to register creative works rather than signature certificates, an office whose mandate covers creative works rather than any signature infrastructure"], answer: 0, exp: "Licensed Certifying Authorities issue digital signature certificates under CCA oversight, not UIDAI or the Copyright Office." },
  { id: "leg-09", topic: "itp-legal", q: "The Digital Personal Data Protection (DPDP) Act, 2023 is best described as:", options: ["A law that applies exclusively to data collected as part of the Aadhaar enrollment process specifically, a scope far narrower than the Act's actual nationwide, sector-wide reach","India's comprehensive law governing how personal data may be processed, centered on consent-based processing and defined obligations for entities handling that data","An amendment specifically inserted into the Copyright Act, 1957, rather than a standalone statute","A state-specific law that is applicable only within the territory of Gujarat"], answer: 1, exp: "The DPDP Act is India's comprehensive, nationwide personal-data-protection law, not limited to Aadhaar data or a single state." },
  { id: "leg-10", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Under the DPDP Act, 2023, the individual whose personal data is being processed is referred to as the:", options: ["Data Fiduciary","Controller of Certifying Authorities","Data Principal","Data Protection Board"], answer: 2, exp: "The individual whose data is processed is termed the Data Principal; the Data Fiduciary is the entity processing that data." },
  { id: "leg-11", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Under the DPDP Act, 2023, the entity that determines the purpose and means of processing personal data, and bears corresponding obligations, is called the:", options: ["Data Principal","Controller of Certifying Authorities","Information Commission","Data Fiduciary"], answer: 3, exp: "The Data Fiduciary determines the purpose/means of processing and carries obligations toward the Data Principal." },
  { id: "leg-12", topic: "itp-legal", q: "The DPDP Act, 2023 establishes which body to handle enforcement and grievances related to personal data protection?", options: ["The Data Protection Board of India","The Controller of Certifying Authorities","UIDAI","The Central Information Commission"], answer: 0, exp: "The Data Protection Board of India is the dedicated enforcement/grievance body created under the DPDP Act." },
  { id: "leg-13", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Under the DPDP Act, 2023, lawful processing of an individual's personal data generally requires:", options: ["No legal basis whatsoever; the Act is understood to let any entity process any personal data freely, a description that overlooks the consent and lawful-basis requirements the Act actually sets out","The individual's (Data Principal's) consent, or another legally recognized basis specified under the Act","Prior approval obtained specifically from the Copyright Office before any processing may begin","Registration completed specifically under the Aadhaar Act, rather than under the DPDP Act itself"], answer: 1, exp: "The DPDP Act requires consent, or another lawful basis defined under the Act, before personal data may be processed." },
  { id: "leg-14", topic: "itp-legal", q: "A 'Significant Data Fiduciary' under the DPDP Act, 2023 refers to:", options: ["Any entity that is described here as never having processed any personal data at all","A term that the Act is said to leave entirely undefined, with no criteria attached to it, a claim that overlooks the specific volume and sensitivity criteria the Act's rules actually set","A category of data fiduciary designated for additional obligations, typically based on factors like the volume/sensitivity of personal data it processes","A category applying only to entities that happen to be located outside India's territory"], answer: 2, exp: "Significant Data Fiduciary is a designated category carrying additional obligations, based on factors such as data volume/sensitivity." },
  { id: "leg-15", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which best distinguishes the DPDP Act, 2023 from the IT Act, 2000's earlier data-related provisions?", options: ["The DPDP Act is, in substance, simply a renamed section carried over from within the IT Act itself","The IT Act's own data-related provisions are, in fact, described here as more comprehensive than the DPDP Act's, a claim that overlooks how narrow the IT Act's own data-related sections actually are","The two laws are said to address completely unrelated subject matter, with no overlap between them at all","The DPDP Act is a dedicated, comprehensive data-protection law with its own consent framework and Data Protection Board, going well beyond the narrower data-related provisions that existed within the IT Act"], answer: 3, exp: "The DPDP Act is a much more comprehensive, dedicated data-protection statute compared to the IT Act's narrower earlier provisions." },
  { id: "leg-16", topic: "itp-legal", q: "The Aadhaar Act, 2016's full formal title reflects its purpose of:", options: ["Targeted delivery of financial and other subsidies, benefits and services to individuals, using Aadhaar-based authentication","General data protection covering every type of personal data processed anywhere in the country, a scope that goes well beyond what this particular statute's own title and provisions actually cover","Software copyright protection, a subject that instead falls under a wholly separate statute","Regulating the registration of internet domain names, a function that instead belongs to NIXI"], answer: 0, exp: "The Aadhaar Act's full title centers on targeted delivery of subsidies, benefits and services via Aadhaar authentication." },
  { id: "leg-17", topic: "itp-legal", q: "UIDAI's authority to issue and manage Aadhaar numbers was placed on a statutory footing by:", options: ["The IT Act, 2000","The Aadhaar Act, 2016","The Copyright Act, 1957","The RTI Act, 2005"], answer: 1, exp: "The Aadhaar Act, 2016 gave UIDAI its statutory authority, replacing its earlier executive-notification basis." },
  { id: "leg-18", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Section 7 of the Aadhaar Act, 2016 specifically enables:", options: ["Criminal prosecution specifically for offences classified as cybercrime under a different part of the Act, a description that overlooks Section 7's own specific targeted-delivery wording","Copyright registration specifically for software source code, a matter handled by the Copyright Office","The use of Aadhaar-based authentication as a condition for receiving certain government subsidies, benefits or services","Filing formal Right to Information requests with a designated public authority"], answer: 2, exp: "Section 7 is the specific provision enabling Aadhaar-based authentication for targeted delivery of subsidies/benefits/services." },
  { id: "leg-19", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which best describes the scope of the Aadhaar Act, 2016 relative to general personal-data protection?", options: ["The Aadhaar Act is described here as fully replacing the need for any separate data-protection law at all","The Aadhaar Act and the DPDP Act are said to cover identical, fully overlapping subject matter in every respect, a claim that overlooks how differently the two statutes define their scope and subject matter","The Aadhaar Act is described here as having no connection whatsoever to personal data of any kind","The Aadhaar Act governs the Aadhaar/UID system and its use for targeted benefit delivery specifically — it does not, by itself, regulate personal-data processing generally, which is the DPDP Act's role"], answer: 3, exp: "The Aadhaar Act is specific to the UID system and targeted benefit delivery, not a substitute for the DPDP Act's general data-protection role." },
  { id: "leg-20", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Computer software/source code in India is generally protected under:", options: ["The Copyright Act, 1957, which treats it as a 'literary work'","The Aadhaar Act, 2016, which governs identity authentication rather than software","The RTI Act, 2005, which governs citizens' access to information","No law in India is understood to protect software of any kind"], answer: 0, exp: "The Copyright Act, 1957 treats computer software/source code as a literary work, giving it copyright protection." },
  { id: "leg-21", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Copyright protection for an original work (including software) under the Copyright Act, 1957 generally:", options: ["Only exists once a work has been formally registered, with no automatic protection available at all, a description that overlooks the Act's own automatic, creation-based protection rule","Arises automatically upon creation of the work, though registration can provide useful evidentiary benefits in disputes","Requires prior government approval to be granted before the software may even be written","Applies only to works that happen to have been created by government employees specifically"], answer: 1, exp: "Copyright protection arises automatically upon creation; formal registration is optional but useful evidentiary support in disputes." },
  { id: "leg-22", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Under Indian copyright law, unauthorized copying of a company's proprietary software source code by a competitor would primarily raise concerns under:", options: ["The Aadhaar Act, 2016, which is generally understood to have no bearing on software copying at all","The RTI Act, 2005, which concerns information requests rather than intellectual-property infringement","The Copyright Act, 1957, since the source code is protected as a literary work","No Indian statute is understood to address this kind of situation at all"], answer: 2, exp: "Copying proprietary source code is primarily a Copyright Act concern, since source code is protected as a literary work." },
  { id: "leg-23", topic: "itp-legal", q: "The Right to Information (RTI) Act, 2005 primarily gives citizens:", options: ["A right to demand free government services, requiring no formal application process of any kind, a description that overlooks the formal request process and exemptions the Act actually sets out","A right to freely access other citizens' private personal data held by public authorities","A right that is said to apply only to central government ministries, and never to state bodies","A legal right to request and receive information from public authorities, subject to defined exemptions and response timelines"], answer: 3, exp: "The RTI Act gives citizens a legal right to information from public authorities, subject to defined exemptions and timelines." },
  { id: "leg-24", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Under the RTI Act, 2005, a public authority is generally required to respond to an information request within:", options: ["A defined statutory time limit (with a shorter timeline where the information concerns a person's life or liberty)","No time limit whatsoever; a response may instead be given entirely at the authority's own convenience, a description that overlooks the defined statutory response timeline the Act actually sets","Exactly one year from the date of filing, with no exceptions carved out anywhere in the Act","Only where the person making the request happens to be a serving government employee"], answer: 0, exp: "RTI has a defined statutory response timeline, with an even shorter timeline for information concerning life or liberty." },
  { id: "leg-25", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Appeals or complaints regarding an unsatisfied RTI request are generally handled by:", options: ["The Controller of Certifying Authorities, which regulates digital-signature infrastructure instead","Central or State Information Commissions, as applicable","UIDAI, which is generally understood to administer Aadhaar rather than RTI appeals","The Data Protection Board of India, which handles DPDP Act grievances instead"], answer: 1, exp: "Information Commissions (Central or State) are the appellate/complaint bodies under the RTI Act." },
  { id: "leg-26", topic: "itp-legal", q: "How does the RTI Act, 2005 connect to e-governance's transparency goals?", options: ["It is described here as having no connection to e-governance whatsoever, despite both sharing accountability goals, a claim that overlooks how often RTI requests now flow through digital government portals","It is described here as replacing the need for any e-governance service delivery of any kind","It gives citizens a formal legal channel to hold public authorities accountable, and RTI requests/responses are increasingly filed and tracked digitally, reinforcing e-governance's transparency objectives","It applies, in this view, only to information about private companies, and never to government bodies"], answer: 2, exp: "RTI reinforces e-governance transparency by giving citizens a formal accountability channel, increasingly delivered through digital portals." },
  { id: "leg-27", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which best distinguishes the RTI Act, 2005 from the DPDP Act, 2023?", options: ["They are, in substance, simply two different names used for referring to the exact same underlying law","The RTI Act protects personal data while the DPDP Act grants access to public information — the reverse of their actual roles, a description that swaps which of the two statutes actually protects data versus grants access","Neither law is described here as having any relevance to e-governance whatsoever, despite both being cited often","The RTI Act is about a citizen's right to obtain information from public authorities; the DPDP Act is about protecting an individual's personal data from misuse — the two address different, sometimes competing, interests (transparency versus privacy)"], answer: 3, exp: "RTI advances transparency/disclosure, while the DPDP Act advances privacy/data protection — related but distinct, sometimes competing, goals." },
  { id: "leg-28", topic: "itp-legal", q: "A fintech app performing Aadhaar-based eKYC (electronic Know Your Customer) to open a new account would rely on which combination of laws for its core operation?", options: ["The IT Act (legal validity of the electronic record/process), the Aadhaar Act (legal basis for Aadhaar-based authentication), and the DPDP Act (consent and data-handling obligations)","Only the Copyright Act, 1957, since no other statute is understood to be relevant to this scenario at all, overlooking how the scenario also depends on Aadhaar-based authentication and consent rules","Only the RTI Act, 2005, since no other statute is understood to be relevant to this scenario at all","No law of any kind is understood to apply to a scenario of this particular type"], answer: 0, exp: "A real-world eKYC flow typically depends jointly on the IT Act, Aadhaar Act and DPDP Act, each governing a different aspect." },
  { id: "leg-29", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which best explains why the IT Act, 2000's cybercrime-specific sections are only briefly mentioned in this topic?", options: ["Because the IT Act is understood to contain no cybercrime-related provisions of any kind whatsoever, a claim contradicted by the Act's own separately numbered chapter on offences and penalties","Those provisions (offences, penalties, the National Cyber Security Policy 2013) are covered in depth in the dedicated Cyber Security unit; this topic instead focuses on the IT Act's civil/commercial role","Because cybercrime in India is instead said to be governed exclusively under the DPDP Act, 2023","Because those cybercrime provisions were formally repealed and are said to no longer exist in law"], answer: 1, exp: "Cybercrime-specific IT Act provisions are covered in depth elsewhere (the Cyber Security unit), so this topic emphasizes the Act's civil/commercial role." },
  { id: "leg-30", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which of these best illustrates the IT Act, 2000's legal-recognition function in practice?", options: ["A court refusing to accept any electronic evidence at all, under any circumstance whatsoever","A government department requiring every digital document to also be separately notarized on paper before it has any legal effect","A court accepting a digitally signed electronic contract as valid evidence, on the same legal footing as a signed paper contract","A private company being exempted entirely from the ordinary operation of contract law altogether"], answer: 2, exp: "This is the core practical effect of the IT Act's legal-recognition provisions — electronic records/signatures standing on equal legal footing with paper." },
  { id: "leg-31", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which of these best illustrates the DPDP Act, 2023's consent-based processing requirement in practice?", options: ["A company collecting and freely reselling any user's personal data, with no notice or agreement obtained at all","A government department publishing a citizen's private medical records without ever seeking their knowledge or consent, though without any consent framework of the kind the DPDP Act itself requires","An app that collects no personal data of any kind at all, making the question of consent effectively irrelevant to it","A mobile app asking a user to explicitly agree before it collects and processes their personal data for a stated purpose"], answer: 3, exp: "Explicit consent before processing personal data for a stated purpose is exactly what the DPDP Act's consent framework requires." },
  { id: "leg-32", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which of these best illustrates the Aadhaar Act, 2016's 'targeted delivery' provision in practice?", options: ["A subsidized cooking-gas scheme requiring Aadhaar-based authentication to confirm the beneficiary's identity before crediting the subsidy","A private company using Aadhaar data for unrelated marketing purposes, without any legal basis under the Act, unlike the lawful, authentication-based use permitted under Section 7 of the Aadhaar Act","A copyright dispute concerning a software product's underlying source code and its authorship","A citizen filing a formal RTI request for information about a particular scheme's annual budget"], answer: 0, exp: "Aadhaar-based authentication as a condition for a targeted subsidy is exactly what Section 7's targeted-delivery provision enables." },
  { id: "leg-33", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which of these best illustrates the Copyright Act, 1957's protection of software in practice?", options: ["A citizen requesting information about the annual budget attached to a particular government scheme, a request handled under the RTI Act rather than any of the other four statutes here","A software company obtaining legal recourse against a competitor who copied significant portions of its proprietary source code without permission","A bank verifying a customer's identity through Aadhaar-based authentication during account opening","A data fiduciary obtaining a user's consent before beginning to process their personal data"], answer: 1, exp: "Legal recourse against unauthorized copying of source code is a direct, practical illustration of the Copyright Act's protection of software." },
  { id: "leg-34", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which of these best illustrates the RTI Act, 2005 in practice?", options: ["A company registering a digital signature certificate through a licensed Certifying Authority","A citizen formally consenting to a mobile app's stated data-processing terms and purpose","A citizen formally requesting, from a government department, details of how funds under a specific welfare scheme were spent","A software company formally registering copyright protection for its own proprietary source code, a step available because source code is treated as a literary work under that Act"], answer: 2, exp: "A formal request to a government department for information on scheme spending is a textbook example of the RTI Act in action." },
  { id: "leg-35", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which best summarizes how the IT Act 2000, DPDP Act 2023, Aadhaar Act 2016, Copyright Act 1957 and RTI Act 2005 together form India's IT legislative framework?", options: ["They are, in substance, five interchangeable versions of essentially the same underlying law, with no distinct scope","Only one of these five Acts is described here as having any practical relevance, with the rest treated as purely symbolic, a claim that overlooks how frequently all five Acts are cited together in practice","They are said to apply only to private companies, carrying no relevance to government IT projects of any kind","Each addresses a distinct but complementary dimension — legal validity of electronic transactions, personal data protection, Aadhaar-specific identity/benefit delivery, software IP protection, and citizen access to information/accountability — collectively enabling and governing India's digital ecosystem"], answer: 3, exp: "Each of these five Acts covers a distinct, complementary dimension of India's overall IT legislative framework." },
  { id: "leg-36", topic: "itp-legal", q: "A government department wants to publish a dataset of aggregated (non-personal) scheme statistics online for public use. Which Act most directly supports this kind of transparency initiative?", options: ["The RTI Act, 2005, whose transparency principle underpins the broader push toward proactive disclosure of government-held information","The Aadhaar Act, 2016, which governs identity authentication rather than the publication of datasets","The Copyright Act, 1957, which protects creative works rather than government transparency initiatives, a statute whose protection runs to authorship rather than to any transparency obligation","No Act in India is understood to have any bearing on a scenario of this particular kind"], answer: 0, exp: "The RTI Act's transparency principle underpins proactive government disclosure initiatives of this kind." },
  { id: "leg-37", topic: "itp-legal", q: "If a Data Fiduciary under the DPDP Act, 2023 fails to meet its obligations regarding a Data Principal's personal data, the matter can be taken up before:", options: ["The Controller of Certifying Authorities exclusively, with no separate enforcement body ever established","The Data Protection Board of India","NIXI, which is generally understood to manage internet exchange points rather than data grievances","GESIA, which is generally understood to be a private industry association rather than a regulator"], answer: 1, exp: "The Data Protection Board of India is the dedicated enforcement body for Data Fiduciary obligation failures under the DPDP Act." },
  { id: "leg-38", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which best describes the legal chain that gives a citizen's DigiLocker-issued certificate the same standing as a paper original?", options: ["The Aadhaar Act, 2016, which is generally understood to have no bearing on document validity at all, a statute whose own scope is limited to identity authentication rather than document validity","The Copyright Act, 1957, which protects authorship rather than the validity of a document itself","The IT Act, 2000's recognition of electronic records and digital/electronic signatures gives the digitally issued, signed document legal validity","No law is understood to give documents of this kind any legal standing whatsoever"], answer: 2, exp: "The IT Act's recognition of electronic records/signatures is the legal basis for DigiLocker documents' validity." },
  { id: "leg-39", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which of the following is NOT one of the five Acts covered in this topic's legislative framework for IT in India?", options: ["The IT Act, 2000, covering legal validity of electronic records and signatures","The Aadhaar Act, 2016, covering identity authentication and targeted benefit delivery specifically","The DPDP Act, 2023, covering consent-based personal data processing obligations","The Indian Contract Act, 1872, which is not specific to IT/electronic transactions and is not covered here"], answer: 3, exp: "The Indian Contract Act, 1872 is a general contract law, not one of the five IT-specific Acts covered in this topic." },
  { id: "leg-40", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Why is it important to distinguish the DPDP Act, 2023 from the RTI Act, 2005 in exam preparation?", options: ["Because they serve opposing-sounding but complementary goals — data protection (limiting disclosure of personal data) versus right to information (enabling disclosure of public-authority information) — and are easy to confuse if not studied carefully","Because the two statutes are, in substance, legally identical and are said to be fully interchangeable with one another, a claim that overlooks how differently disclosure and protection duties are actually structured","Because, in this view, only one of the two Acts is understood to actually exist in force today","Because neither Act is understood to carry any relevance to e-governance whatsoever, despite being cited often"], answer: 0, exp: "These two Acts pursue opposite-sounding goals (limiting versus enabling disclosure), which is exactly why careful distinction matters." },
  { id: "leg-41", topic: "itp-legal", q: "A Certifying Authority operating under the IT Act, 2000's regulatory structure is licensed and overseen by:", options: ["The Data Protection Board of India, which handles DPDP Act matters rather than Certifying Authorities","The Controller of Certifying Authorities (CCA)","UIDAI, which is generally understood to administer Aadhaar rather than digital-signature licensing","The Central Information Commission, which is generally understood to handle RTI appeals instead"], answer: 1, exp: "The Controller of Certifying Authorities licenses and oversees Certifying Authorities under the IT Act." },
  { id: "leg-42", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which best explains why the DPDP Act, 2023 was needed even though the IT Act, 2000 already existed?", options: ["The IT Act had, in this view, already fully addressed personal data protection, leaving no meaningful gaps at all, a reading that overlooks how narrow the IT Act's own original data provisions actually were","The DPDP Act was, in this view, needed mainly in order to repeal the Aadhaar Act entirely","The IT Act's data-related provisions were comparatively narrow, and India needed a comprehensive, dedicated statute specifically addressing consent-based personal data processing and providing a specialized enforcement body","No additional data-protection legislation was, in this view, actually needed at any point"], answer: 2, exp: "The IT Act's data provisions were comparatively narrow, motivating a dedicated, comprehensive statute like the DPDP Act." },
  { id: "leg-43", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which Act specifically established the legal basis for a citizen's Aadhaar number to be used for authenticating eligibility for a subsidized government scheme?", options: ["The Copyright Act, 1957, which is generally understood to concern creative works rather than identity authentication","The RTI Act, 2005, which is generally understood to concern citizens' access to information rather than authentication","The IT Act, 2000, which does not itself establish Aadhaar's legal basis","The Aadhaar Act, 2016"], answer: 3, exp: "The Aadhaar Act, 2016 specifically provides the legal basis for Aadhaar-based authentication in targeted benefit delivery." },
  { id: "leg-44", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which Act would a company primarily rely on to legally protect its mobile application's underlying source code from being copied by a competitor?", options: ["The Copyright Act, 1957","The Aadhaar Act, 2016, which governs identity authentication rather than software protection","The RTI Act, 2005, which governs access to information rather than intellectual-property protection","The DPDP Act, 2023, which governs personal-data processing rather than software copying"], answer: 0, exp: "The Copyright Act, 1957 is the primary source of legal protection for a company's software source code." },
  { id: "leg-45", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which Act would a citizen rely on to request a copy of the feasibility study behind a proposed government e-governance project?", options: ["The Copyright Act, 1957, which protects creative and literary works rather than access to information","The RTI Act, 2005","The Aadhaar Act, 2016, which governs identity authentication rather than access to government records","The IT Act, 2000, which does not itself grant a right to request government information"], answer: 1, exp: "The RTI Act, 2005 grants citizens the right to request such information from a public authority." },
  { id: "leg-46", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which Act would primarily govern whether an e-commerce company can lawfully use a customer's browsing history to personalize offers, absent any specific consent obtained for that purpose?", options: ["The Copyright Act, 1957, which protects creative works rather than the processing of personal data","The RTI Act, 2005, which concerns access to public-authority information rather than data processing","The DPDP Act, 2023","The Aadhaar Act, 2016, which is specific to Aadhaar-based authentication, not general data use"], answer: 2, exp: "The DPDP Act, 2023 governs the lawful basis for processing personal data such as browsing history, including where consent is required." },
  { id: "leg-47", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which Act gives a digitally signed loan agreement, executed entirely online, the same legal standing as a signed paper agreement?", options: ["The RTI Act, 2005, which concerns access to public-authority information rather than contract validity","The Copyright Act, 1957, which protects creative works rather than the validity of an electronic agreement","The Aadhaar Act, 2016, which governs identity authentication rather than the general validity of electronic contracts","The IT Act, 2000"], answer: 3, exp: "The IT Act, 2000's recognition of electronic records/signatures gives a digitally signed online agreement equal legal standing to a paper one." },
  { id: "leg-48", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Overall, which best describes the purpose of studying India's legislative framework for IT together, rather than each Act in isolation?", options: ["Because a single real-world digital service (like an eKYC-based fintech app or a DigiLocker-issued certificate) often depends on several of these Acts working together — e.g. the IT Act for legal validity, the Aadhaar Act for authentication, and the DPDP Act for data-handling obligations","Because these five Acts are said to have no practical relationship to one another whatsoever, despite frequently applying together, a claim that overlooks how often eKYC and similar services draw on several of them together","Because, in this view, only a single one of these Acts is ever actually relevant to any real digital service","Because, in this view, studying these Acts together is explicitly discouraged as a matter of settled legal practice"], answer: 0, exp: "Real digital services typically depend on several of these Acts working together, which is why they are best studied as a connected framework." },
  { id: "leg-49", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Which best captures the difference in emphasis between the IT Act, 2000 and the DPDP Act, 2023?", options: ["They are, in substance, functionally identical laws that happen to have been passed in the very same year","The IT Act broadly enables lawful electronic transactions and recognizes electronic records/signatures; the DPDP Act specifically protects individuals' personal data during processing, with its own consent framework and enforcement board","The IT Act is, in this view, about personal data protection while the DPDP Act is about digital signatures — the reverse of their actual focus, a description that swaps which statute actually governs signatures versus personal data","Neither Act is understood, in this view, to carry any relevance to real-world digital services at all"], answer: 1, exp: "The IT Act enables lawful electronic transactions broadly, while the DPDP Act specifically protects personal data during processing." },
  { id: "leg-50", topic: "itp-legal", q: "Overall, the IT Act 2000's civil/commercial provisions, the DPDP Act 2023, the Aadhaar Act 2016, the Copyright Act 1957's software protection, and the RTI Act 2005 together illustrate:", options: ["An arbitrary, unrelated collection of laws that are said to share no common relevance to IT projects at all","A framework said to apply only to private companies, carrying no bearing on government e-governance projects at all, a claim that overlooks how directly government e-governance projects rely on several of these Acts","A legislative framework where the legal validity of digital transactions, protection of personal data, a specific identity/benefit-delivery system, protection of software as intellectual property, and citizen access to information collectively enable — and hold accountable — India's expanding digital government and IT ecosystem","A single Act that is, in this view, effectively repeated across five different names"], answer: 2, exp: "Together, these five Acts both enable and hold accountable India's expanding digital government and IT ecosystem." }

);
